(window.SLV_PACKS = window.SLV_PACKS || []).push({"id":"intrusion","name":"Intrusion","blurb":"Burglary alarm, residential to small commercial","calculators":["battery","drop","gauge"],"openTips":7,"learn":{"modules":[{"num":1,"title":"Foundations","introHtml":"<p><strong>Who it's for:</strong> brand-new techs, or anyone who wants a refresher on electrical basics. <strong>You'll be able to:</strong> use Ohm's law on the job, tell series from parallel circuits, explain what makes a circuit Class 2, pick the right tools, and stay safe on site.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Voltage, current, and resistance","html":"<p>Every alarm circuit comes down to three numbers.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Unit</th><th>Think of it as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>V (or E)</td><td>volts (V)</td><td>The push. How hard electricity is being pushed through the wire.</td></tr><tr><td>Current</td><td>I</td><td>amps (A), milliamps (mA)</td><td>The flow. How much electricity is actually moving.</td></tr><tr><td>Resistance</td><td>R</td><td>ohms (Ω)</td><td>The restriction. How hard the path is to push through.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>1 amp = 1,000 milliamps. Alarm devices are usually rated in mA (a contact draws nothing, a motion detector draws roughly 10 to 30 mA, a keypad roughly 30 to 150 mA). Always check the device's spec sheet.</p>\n<h4>Ohm's law</h4>\n<p><strong>V = I × R</strong></p>\n<p>Cover the one you want to find:</p>\n<ul><li>Voltage = Current × Resistance</li><li>Current = Voltage ÷ Resistance</li><li>Resistance = Voltage ÷ Current</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Power:</strong> P = V × I, measured in watts (W).</p>\n<p><strong>Example.</strong> A siren draws 0.5 A on a 12 V circuit. Power = 12 × 0.5 = 6 W.</p>\n<p><strong>Example.</strong> A zone has a 2,200 Ω (2.2 kΩ) end-of-line resistor and the panel puts about 5 V on the loop. Current = 5 ÷ 2,200 ≈ 0.0023 A, or 2.3 mA. This tiny current is how the panel &quot;watches&quot; the zone.</p>\n<h4>AC vs DC</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>AC (alternating current)</strong> reverses direction many times a second. The transformer that plugs into the wall puts out low-voltage AC (commonly 16.5 VAC or 18 VAC) to the panel.</li><li><strong>DC (direct current)</strong> flows one way. The panel converts the AC to DC (usually about 12 to 13.8 VDC) to run devices and charge the battery.</li></ul>\n<p>When you meter a panel's AC input terminals, set the meter to AC volts. Everywhere else in the system (aux power, bell output, battery) is DC.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"1.2","title":"Series and parallel","html":"<p><strong>Series:</strong> devices connected end to end, one path. The same current flows through all of them. If any one opens, the whole circuit opens.</p>\n<ul><li>This is how normally closed door contacts are wired on one zone: open any door and the loop breaks.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Parallel:</strong> devices connected across the same two wires, several paths. Each device gets the same voltage. If one opens, the others keep working.</p>\n<ul><li>This is how you power several motion detectors from aux power: each gets 12 V, and their currents add up.</li><li>This is how normally open devices (like some panic buttons) are wired on one zone: close any one and the loop shorts.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Resistors in series add:</strong> 1 kΩ + 1 kΩ = 2 kΩ. <strong>Two equal resistors in parallel halve:</strong> 2 kΩ in parallel with 2 kΩ = 1 kΩ.</p>\n<p>You'll use those two rules constantly when you read a zone with a meter (see <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/4\">Module 4</a>).</p>","mins":2},{"id":"1.3","title":"Class 2 and power-limited circuits","html":"<p>Most intrusion wiring is <strong>Class 2</strong>, a category in the National Electrical Code (NEC) for circuits whose power source is limited so they don't present a significant fire or shock risk.</p>\n<p>What that means for you:</p>\n<ul><li>Panels, transformers, and power supplies are listed as Class 2 sources, and their output is limited (typically no more than 100 VA for the low voltages we use).</li><li>Class 2 wiring has lighter rules than power wiring, but it still has rules: use listed cable (CL2, CL3, or a higher rating like CL2R/CL2P where required), keep it separated from line-voltage wiring, and support it properly.</li><li><strong>Never</strong> run Class 2 cable in the same raceway or box as 120 V wiring unless a listed barrier separates them.</li><li>Plenum spaces (air-handling ceilings) require plenum-rated cable (CL2P or CMP).</li></ul>\n<p>Local codes and the AHJ (authority having jurisdiction) can add requirements. See <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/8\">Module 8</a>.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"1.4","title":"Tools of the trade","html":"<p><strong>Must have</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Digital multimeter (volts AC/DC, ohms, continuity, ideally DC milliamps)</li><li>Wire strippers sized for 22 to 14 AWG</li><li>Screwdrivers, including a small flat &quot;tweaker&quot; for terminal strips</li><li>Drill/driver, bits, and a long flex bit for fishing walls</li><li>Fish tape and glow rods</li><li>Cable staples/straps, wire nuts or butt connectors (gel-filled for damp areas)</li><li>Label maker or wire markers</li><li>Ladder (rated for your weight plus tools)</li><li>Flashlight or headlamp</li><li>Smartphone with installer app/manufacturer software</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Nice to have</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Toner and probe (to trace unlabeled wires)</li><li>Inspection camera</li><li>Magnet set (to test contacts and find gap)</li><li>Glass break simulator</li><li>Walk-test light or second person on the phone</li><li>Cell signal meter</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the one tool new techs forget, and why it matters.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"1.5","title":"Job site safety","html":"<ul><li><strong>Ladders:</strong> three points of contact, never stand on the top two steps, set extension ladders at a 4:1 angle.</li><li><strong>Attics and crawlspaces:</strong> step only on joists, watch for nails coming through the roof deck, wear a mask around insulation, and stay hydrated in hot attics.</li><li><strong>Line voltage:</strong> you're a low-voltage tech. Don't open electrical panels or work on 120 V circuits unless you're licensed for it. When a transformer needs a dedicated outlet, that's an electrician's job.</li><li><strong>Drilling:</strong> check for wires, pipes, and gas lines before you drill. Use an inspection camera or stud finder with AC detection.</li><li><strong>Batteries:</strong> sealed lead-acid batteries can deliver a lot of current. Don't short the terminals with a tool. Disconnect the battery before working on the panel board.</li><li><strong>Customer homes:</strong> shoe covers, drop cloths, clean up dust, ask before moving furniture.</li><li><strong>Personal safety:</strong> in vacant or under-construction buildings, let your office know where you are.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"1.6","title":"Reading a basic floor plan","html":"<p>Before you design or install, you need to read the building.</p>\n<ul><li>Find the <strong>scale</strong> (for example 1/4&quot; = 1'). Use it to estimate wire runs.</li><li>Identify <strong>perimeter openings</strong>: exterior doors, windows, garage doors, basement access.</li><li>Identify <strong>interior traffic paths</strong>: hallways and rooms an intruder must pass through. That's where motion detectors go.</li><li>Find a <strong>panel location</strong>: usually a closet, basement, or utility room, near an outlet, out of sight from entry doors, and with a reasonable path for wiring.</li><li>Mark the <strong>entry/exit door</strong> the customer uses most. The keypad goes there.</li></ul>\n<p>Common symbols you'll mark on a plan: C (contact), M or PIR (motion), GB (glass break), K (keypad), S (siren), P (panel), SD (smoke detector).</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["A device draws 250 mA. How many amps is that?","A 12 V circuit has 0.2 A flowing. How many watts is that?","Five door contacts are wired one after another on one loop. Is that series or parallel?","You have two 1 kΩ resistors in series. What's the total resistance?","What meter setting do you use on a panel's transformer input terminals?","True or false: Class 2 cable can share a junction box with 120 V wiring if you're careful.","What cable rating do you need in an air-handling (plenum) ceiling?","Name two things to check for before drilling into a wall."],"answers":["0.25 A","2.4 W","Series","2 kΩ","AC volts","False, not without a listed barrier","Plenum rated (CL2P or CMP)","Any two of: electrical wires, water pipes, gas lines, HVAC ducts"],"keyHtml":"1) 0.25 A. 2) 2.4 W. 3) Series. 4) 2 kΩ. 5) AC volts. 6) False, not without a listed barrier. 7) Plenum rated (CL2P or CMP). 8) Any two of: electrical wires, water pipes, gas lines, HVAC ducts.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":2,"title":"How an Intrusion System Works","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> name every major part of an alarm system, explain zones and partitions, and trace an alarm from the sensor to the police dispatcher.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"2.1","title":"The parts of a system","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Control panel</strong></td><td>The brain. A circuit board in a metal can with a transformer and battery. It watches every zone, decides what's an alarm, sounds the siren, and sends signals out.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Keypad</strong></td><td>The user interface. Arms, disarms, shows status, and is where you program the system. Some systems use a touchscreen or phone app instead of or alongside it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sensors (initiating devices)</strong></td><td>Detect something happening: a door opening, motion, breaking glass.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Siren / sounder</strong></td><td>Makes noise inside (and sometimes outside) on alarm.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Communicator</strong></td><td>Sends alarm signals to the central station: cellular, internet (IP), or phone line.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Power</strong></td><td>Transformer (AC from the wall) plus a backup battery inside the panel.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Expanders / modules</strong></td><td>Add zones, wireless receivers, outputs, or more power.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","mins":2},{"id":"2.2","title":"Zones","html":"<p>A <strong>zone</strong> is an input the panel watches. It might be one device (a motion detector) or several devices wired together (all the windows in a bedroom).</p>\n<p>Each zone has:</p>\n<ul><li>A <strong>number</strong> and a <strong>name</strong> (&quot;Zone 3, Kitchen Windows&quot;)</li><li>A <strong>zone type</strong> that tells the panel how to react (entry/exit, perimeter, interior, 24-hour; see <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/7\">Module 7</a>)</li><li><strong>Attributes</strong> like chime, bypassable, or swinger shutdown</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> the more zones you use, the more precise the central station's report is. &quot;Zone 7, Back Door&quot; helps police more than &quot;Zone 1, All Doors and Windows&quot;. Many techs put each door on its own zone and group windows by room.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: how you decide when to combine devices on one zone.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"2.3","title":"Partitions","html":"<p>A <strong>partition</strong> (also called an area) splits one panel into separate systems that arm and disarm independently.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul><li>A house with a detached garage or in-law apartment</li><li>A small business with a front office and a warehouse</li><li>A strip of two storefronts sharing one panel</li></ul>\n<p>Each partition has its own zones, user codes, and keypads. A user can be given access to one partition or several.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"2.4","title":"Arming modes","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Common names</th><th>What's armed</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Away</td><td>Away, Full</td><td>Perimeter and interior</td><td>Nobody home</td></tr><tr><td>Stay</td><td>Stay, Home, Perimeter</td><td>Perimeter only; interior motion is ignored</td><td>People home, moving around</td></tr><tr><td>Night</td><td>Night, Night-Stay</td><td>Perimeter plus selected interior zones</td><td>Everyone in bed; downstairs motion armed</td></tr><tr><td>Instant</td><td>Instant, Max</td><td>Like Stay or Away but no entry delay</td><td>Extra-secure when nobody should come in</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","mins":2},{"id":"2.5","title":"The signal path","html":"<p>What happens when a burglar opens the back door while the system is armed Away:</p>\n<ol><li>The <strong>contact</strong> on the door opens.</li><li>The <strong>zone</strong> loop resistance changes, and the panel sees it.</li><li>The zone is an <strong>entry/exit</strong> type, so the panel starts the <strong>entry delay</strong> and the keypad beeps.</li><li>Nobody enters a code. The entry delay expires.</li><li>The panel goes into <strong>alarm</strong>: the siren sounds.</li><li>The panel holds the signal for a short <strong>abort window</strong> (if programmed) in case the user disarms late.</li><li>The <strong>communicator</strong> sends the alarm (account number, event code, zone number) to the <strong>central station</strong> over cellular or IP.</li><li>The <strong>central station receiver</strong> decodes it and puts it on an operator's screen.</li><li>The operator follows the account's <strong>action plan</strong>: typically call the premises and/or keyholders to verify (Enhanced Call Verification), then dispatch police if it isn't canceled.</li><li>The siren times out after the programmed <strong>bell cutoff</strong> time and the system resets, ready to alarm again.</li></ol>","mins":2},{"id":"2.6","title":"Supervision and troubles","html":"<p>The panel doesn't only watch for alarms. It also watches itself and reports <strong>troubles</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li>AC power loss</li><li>Low or missing battery</li><li>Communication failure</li><li>Zone tamper or wire fault</li><li>Wireless sensor supervision loss or low battery</li><li>Keypad or module communication loss</li></ul>\n<p>Troubles usually make the keypad beep and show a message, and many are reported to the central station. Clearing troubles is a big part of service work (see the Troubleshoot tab).</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What part of the system decides whether something is an alarm?","What's the difference between a zone and a partition?","In Stay mode, which zones are usually ignored?","List the steps from &quot;door opens&quot; to &quot;operator sees the signal&quot;.","What does Enhanced Call Verification mean?","Name three troubles a panel supervises."],"answers":["The control panel","A zone is one monitored input; a partition is a group of zones that arms and disarms as its own system","Interior (motion) zones","Contact opens, zone changes, entry delay, delay expires, alarm, communicator sends signal, receiver decodes, operator screen","The central station makes at least two calls to verify before dispatching","Any three from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/2/2.6\">Lesson 2.6</a>"],"keyHtml":"1) The control panel. 2) A zone is one monitored input; a partition is a group of zones that arms and disarms as its own system. 3) Interior (motion) zones. 4) Contact opens, zone changes, entry delay, delay expires, alarm, communicator sends signal, receiver decodes, operator screen. 5) The central station makes at least two calls to verify before dispatching. 6) Any three from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/2/2.6\">Lesson 2.6</a>.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":3,"title":"Sensors and Devices","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> choose the right sensor for each opening or area, explain how each one detects, and avoid the placement mistakes that cause false alarms.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"3.1","title":"Door and window contacts","html":"<p>A contact is a <strong>reed switch</strong> plus a <strong>magnet</strong>. The switch mounts on the frame, the magnet on the moving door or window. When they're together, the switch is closed (on a normally closed contact). Separate them and it opens.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Where it's used</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Surface</strong></td><td>Doors and windows where you can't drill</td><td>Easy to install, visible, can be knocked off</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Recessed</strong> (plunger-style bore)</td><td>Wood doors and windows</td><td>Hidden, durable; drill frame and door in line with each other</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Overhead door</strong></td><td>Garage and roll-up doors</td><td>Heavy-duty, armored cable, wide gap; floor mount or track mount</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wide-gap</strong></td><td>Loose-fitting doors, steel doors</td><td>Stronger magnet allows a bigger gap</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Gap</strong> is the distance at which the switch opens. Typical surface contacts operate around 3/4&quot; to 1&quot; gap; overhead door contacts are rated for 1.5&quot; to 2&quot; or more. <strong>Steel doors and frames reduce gap</strong>, sometimes by half; use contacts rated for steel.</p>\n<p><strong>Common mistakes</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Magnet and switch misaligned, so the zone flickers when the door rattles in wind</li><li>Recessed holes not lined up, so the door has to slam to close the zone</li><li>Using a standard contact on a steel door</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"3.2","title":"Motion detectors (PIR)","html":"<p>A <strong>passive infrared (PIR)</strong> detector doesn't send anything out. It watches for a <strong>change in infrared heat</strong> across its zones. A body-temperature object moving from one detection zone to the next triggers it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key point:</strong> PIRs are most sensitive to motion <strong>across</strong> the pattern, and least sensitive to someone walking straight <strong>toward</strong> the detector. Aim them so an intruder would cross the coverage.</p>\n<p><strong>Mounting</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Most wall-mounted PIRs are designed for a specific height, commonly about 7 to 7.5 feet. Mounting at the wrong height shrinks coverage or creates dead zones.</li><li>Corner mounting gives the widest coverage of a room.</li><li>Ceiling-mount 360° PIRs are good for open areas and rooms with lots of obstructions.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Avoid aiming at</strong> (these cause false alarms)</p>\n<ul><li>Heat sources: furnaces, space heaters, fireplaces, wood stoves</li><li>Air vents blowing directly on the detector</li><li>Windows getting direct sunlight</li><li>Moving objects: ceiling fans, curtains near vents, balloons</li><li>Stairs that a large dog can climb (even with pet-immune models)</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Pet immunity</strong> lets animals up to a rated weight (often 40 to 80 lb) move without triggering, but only when mounted exactly as the manufacturer specifies, and only if pets can't climb above the lower detection zone.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"3.3","title":"Dual-technology motion","html":"<p>A <strong>dual-tech</strong> detector combines PIR with <strong>microwave</strong> (which bounces radio waves and senses motion by Doppler shift). Both technologies must trip at the same time to alarm.</p>\n<ul><li>Much more resistant to false alarms in tough environments (garages, warehouses, rooms with heat sources)</li><li>Microwave goes through glass and thin walls, so aim it carefully and adjust its range so it doesn't see outside</li><li>Draws more current than a plain PIR, so account for it in your battery calculation</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"3.4","title":"Glass break detectors","html":"<p>An <strong>acoustic glass break</strong> detector listens for the sound signature of glass breaking: typically a low-frequency &quot;flex&quot; (the thud of the impact) followed by a high-frequency shatter.</p>\n<ul><li>Covers several windows in a room at once, often up to about 20 to 25 feet</li><li>Mount on a wall or ceiling with a clear line of sight to the glass</li><li>Heavy drapes or shutters block the sound; plan for them</li><li>Test with a <strong>glass break simulator</strong> made for that detector, not by clapping or banging</li><li>Doesn't detect a window that's opened or a pane removed quietly; pair it with contacts or motion</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Shock (vibration) sensors</strong> mount on the glass or frame and detect the impact itself. Useful on single panes or where a room is noisy.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"3.5","title":"Smoke and heat on a burglar panel","html":"<p>Many residential panels also accept smoke and heat detectors. Key differences from intrusion devices:</p>\n<ul><li>Use devices listed for that specific panel (4-wire smoke detectors need a resettable power output; 2-wire smokes need a compatible zone)</li><li>Fire zones are 24-hour and are always active, armed or not</li><li>Fire signals get priority at the central station</li><li>Residential fire installed on a burglar panel still has code requirements (NFPA 72 chapter on household signaling)</li></ul>\n<p>Full fire alarm coverage comes in the Fire pack later. For now: if it's fire, follow the panel manual exactly and check with your supervisor.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"3.6","title":"Panic, hold-up, and environmental","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>What it does</th><th>Zone type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Panic button</strong></td><td>User presses for help; audible or silent</td><td>24-hour audible or silent panic</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hold-up / duress button</strong></td><td>Silent robbery alarm at a business counter</td><td>24-hour silent hold-up</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Medical pendant</strong></td><td>Wireless button for medical emergency</td><td>24-hour medical</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Water / flood sensor</strong></td><td>Detects water on the floor</td><td>24-hour supervisory or auxiliary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Low temperature</strong></td><td>Freeze warning in vacation homes</td><td>24-hour supervisory</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CO detector</strong></td><td>Carbon monoxide</td><td>24-hour CO (use listed devices)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hold-up buttons must be placed where staff can reach them without being noticed, and the signal must be silent. Make sure the customer knows the difference between panic and hold-up.</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What are the two parts of a door contact?","What happens to a contact's gap on a steel door?","A PIR is least sensitive to motion in which direction?","Name three things you shouldn't aim a PIR at.","Why do dual-tech detectors false alarm less?","What two sounds does an acoustic glass break listen for?","Why shouldn't you test a glass break by clapping?","What zone type does a hold-up button use?"],"answers":["Reed switch and magnet","It shrinks","Walking straight toward it","Any three from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/3/3.2\">Lesson 3.2</a>","Both PIR and microwave must trip together","The low-frequency flex and the high-frequency shatter","It doesn't reproduce the glass break signature; use the listed simulator","24-hour silent hold-up"],"keyHtml":"1) Reed switch and magnet. 2) It shrinks. 3) Walking straight toward it. 4) Any three from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/3/3.2\">Lesson 3.2</a>. 5) Both PIR and microwave must trip together. 6) The low-frequency flex and the high-frequency shatter. 7) It doesn't reproduce the glass break signature; use the listed simulator. 8) 24-hour silent hold-up.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":4,"title":"Wiring and Circuits","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> wire zones in every common style, read a zone with a meter, size wire, power devices correctly, and leave a clean, labeled install.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"4.1","title":"Why end-of-line resistors exist","html":"<p>Without a resistor, a panel can only tell &quot;closed&quot; from &quot;open&quot;. A cut wire looks exactly like an open door, and a burglar who shorts the wires together makes the zone look normal forever.</p>\n<p>An <strong>end-of-line (EOL) resistor</strong> placed at the <strong>last device</strong> on the loop gives the panel a known resistance to watch for. Now the panel can tell three states apart:</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>What the panel reads</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>About the EOL value</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>Open (infinite resistance)</td><td>Alarm or trouble, depending on wiring style</td></tr><tr><td>Short (near 0 Ω)</td><td>Alarm or trouble, depending on wiring style</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The resistor goes at the end of the run, at the last device, not at the panel.</strong> A resistor installed at the panel terminals supervises nothing; the panel can't see a cut or short in the wire. Some inspectors and companies require it at the device for this reason.</p>\n<p>EOL values depend on the panel brand. Common values include 1 kΩ, 2 kΩ, 2.2 kΩ, 4.7 kΩ, 5.6 kΩ, and 10 kΩ. <strong>Always use the value in the panel's manual.</strong></p>","mins":2},{"id":"4.2","title":"Zone wiring styles","html":"<h4>Normally closed (NC), single EOL</h4>\n<p>Contacts wired in <strong>series</strong>, resistor in series at the last device.</p>\n<ul><li>Normal: EOL value</li><li>Any device opens or wire cut: open → alarm</li><li>Wires shorted together: near 0 Ω → panel sees trouble or alarm (depends on panel)</li></ul>\n<p>This is the most common style for doors and windows.</p>\n<h4>Normally open (NO), single EOL</h4>\n<p>Devices wired in <strong>parallel</strong>, resistor across the loop at the last device.</p>\n<ul><li>Normal: EOL value</li><li>Any device closes: short → alarm</li><li>Wire cut: open → trouble</li></ul>\n<p>Used for some panic buttons, older glass breaks, and some environmental sensors.</p>\n<h4>Double end-of-line (DEOL)</h4>\n<p>Each device gets <strong>two resistors</strong>: one in series with the loop and one across (in parallel with) the contact. Many panels use two resistors of the same value.</p>\n<p>With two equal resistors of value R:</p>\n<ul><li>Contact closed (normal): the parallel resistor is shorted out, so the panel reads <strong>R</strong></li><li>Contact open (alarm): both resistors are in series, so the panel reads <strong>2R</strong></li><li>Wire cut or tamper opened: <strong>open</strong> → tamper/trouble</li><li>Wires shorted: <strong>near 0 Ω</strong> → fault</li></ul>\n<p>DEOL lets the panel tell an alarm from a tamper or wire fault on the same zone. Check the manual for the exact resistor values and arrangement.</p>\n<h4>No EOL (closed loop)</h4>\n<p>Some panels let you disable EOL supervision. Normal = short, alarm = open. It's quick but unsupervised; avoid it unless the panel or job requires it.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the wiring style you see most often in the field, and how to spot it when you take over another company's system.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"4.3","title":"Reading a zone with a meter","html":"<ol><li>Disconnect the zone wires from the panel terminals (or test at the device end).</li><li>Set the meter to ohms.</li><li>Measure across the two zone wires.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>You read</th><th>Single EOL NC loop means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EOL value (within about 10%)</td><td>Zone is good and closed</td></tr><tr><td>OL / open</td><td>A device is open, a splice failed, or a wire is broken</td></tr><tr><td>Near 0 Ω</td><td>Short somewhere (staple through the cable, pinched wire, wrong wiring)</td></tr><tr><td>A different value</td><td>Wrong resistor, two resistors, or corrosion/bad splice adding resistance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Also check for ground faults:</strong> measure from each zone wire to earth ground. You should read open. Any reading means a wire is touching something grounded.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"4.4","title":"Wire types and gauges","html":"<p><strong>AWG</strong> (American Wire Gauge): a smaller number is a thicker wire.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Typical use</th><th>Common cable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Door/window contacts, zones</td><td>22/2 (22 AWG, 2 conductors)</td></tr><tr><td>Motion detectors (zone + power)</td><td>22/4</td></tr><tr><td>Keypads and data bus</td><td>22/4 (some panels allow or require 18/4 on long runs)</td></tr><tr><td>Sirens and bells</td><td>18/2</td></tr><tr><td>Power supply runs, long device power runs</td><td>18/2 or 18/4, heavier as needed</td></tr><tr><td>Transformer to panel</td><td>18/2 or 16/2 per manual</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Use <strong>stranded</strong> wire where it flexes and <strong>solid</strong> where your terminals and practice call for it; follow the manufacturer.</li><li>Use the <strong>correct jacket rating</strong> for the space (CL2, CL2R riser, CL2P plenum, outdoor/direct-burial rated outside).</li><li>Leave a service loop at the panel and devices.</li></ul>\n<p>Use the <strong>Wire Gauge</strong> and <strong>Voltage Drop</strong> calculators for long runs.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"4.5","title":"Home runs vs data bus","html":"<p><strong>Home run (hardwired zones):</strong> each zone has its own cable back to the panel or a zone expander.</p>\n<p><strong>Data bus (keypad bus, addressable):</strong> keypads, zone expanders, wireless receivers, and output modules share one 4-wire bus: two for power, two for data. Each device needs a unique <strong>address</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bus rules to remember (exact limits vary by panel; check the manual):</p>\n<ul><li>Total bus wire length and per-device length limits</li><li>Star (home-run) vs daisy-chain topology allowed</li><li>Don't run the bus next to AC power or lighting ballasts</li><li>Wire colors vary by manufacturer; match the terminal labels, not the color you're used to</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"4.6","title":"Powering devices","html":"<p>The panel has an <strong>auxiliary (aux) power output</strong> (typically around 12 VDC) with a maximum current rating. Every powered device (motion detectors, keypads, glass breaks, modules) adds to that load.</p>\n<p><strong>Rules</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Add up the <strong>standby current</strong> of everything on aux. It must stay under the panel's aux rating.</li><li>Add up the <strong>alarm current</strong> too (sirens and bell output have their own rating).</li><li>Your battery must carry the standby load for the required hours plus the alarm load for the required minutes (use the <strong>Battery Standby</strong> calculator).</li><li>If you exceed the panel's rating, add a <strong>supervised auxiliary power supply</strong> with its own transformer and battery.</li><li><strong>Fused or PTC-protected outputs</strong> trip on overload; a dead keypad or motion bank can be a tripped aux output.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"4.7","title":"Grounding, wire management, and labeling","html":"<ul><li><strong>Earth ground:</strong> connect the panel's earth-ground terminal to a proper ground (often a cold water pipe or ground rod per manufacturer and local code). It protects the panel from lightning and static.</li><li><strong>Separation:</strong> keep low-voltage cable away from 120 V runs, fluorescent fixtures, and motors. Cross power wiring at right angles.</li><li><strong>Support:</strong> use cable staples sized for the cable and don't pinch it. A staple through the jacket causes intermittent shorts that are hard to find.</li><li><strong>Splices:</strong> solder or use gel-filled connectors. Avoid twisted-and-taped splices.</li><li><strong>Labeling:</strong> label every cable at both ends with the zone number or device. Leave a zone list and wiring chart in the panel can.</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Why does the EOL resistor go at the last device and not at the panel?","On a single EOL NC zone, what does a cut wire look like to the panel?","With DEOL using two 5.6 kΩ resistors, what does the panel read when the door is closed? When it's open?","You meter a zone and read 0 Ω. What's the most likely cause?","Which is thicker, 18 AWG or 22 AWG?","What is the bus used for?","What do you do when total aux current exceeds the panel's rating?","Why should every cable be labeled at both ends?"],"answers":["At the panel, it can't supervise the wire between the panel and the devices","An open, the same as an alarm","Closed: 5.6 kΩ. Open: 11.2 kΩ","A short (staple, pinch, or miswire)","18 AWG","Connecting keypads and modules to the panel over shared power and data wires","Add a supervised auxiliary power supply","So you and the next tech can trace and service the system quickly"],"keyHtml":"1) At the panel, it can't supervise the wire between the panel and the devices. 2) An open, the same as an alarm. 3) Closed: 5.6 kΩ. Open: 11.2 kΩ. 4) A short (staple, pinch, or miswire). 5) 18 AWG. 6) Connecting keypads and modules to the panel over shared power and data wires. 7) Add a supervised auxiliary power supply. 8) So you and the next tech can trace and service the system quickly.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":5,"title":"Wireless Systems","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> explain how wireless sensors talk to a panel, enroll and test them, plan for range, and manage battery life.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"5.1","title":"How wireless works","html":"<p>A wireless sensor is a normal sensor (contact, PIR, smoke) with a small <strong>radio transmitter</strong> and a battery. When it changes state, it sends a coded message to a <strong>receiver</strong> that's built into the panel or connected on the bus.</p>\n<p>Each sensor has a unique <strong>serial number</strong> (also called the TXID or ESN). You enroll that number into a zone so the panel knows which sensor is which.</p>\n<p>Two broad kinds:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>One-way:</strong> the sensor transmits; the panel can't talk back. Simpler and cheaper.</li><li><strong>Two-way:</strong> the panel confirms each message and can send commands back (for example, change sensitivity). More reliable, better battery management.</li></ul>\n<p>Wireless sensors from one manufacturer generally only work with that manufacturer's receivers, and different series from the same brand may not mix. Confirm compatibility before ordering.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.2","title":"Supervision","html":"<p>Because a wireless sensor isn't wired, the panel can't measure a loop. Instead, each sensor sends a periodic <strong>check-in</strong> (supervision) signal. If the panel doesn't hear a sensor within its <strong>supervision window</strong>, it shows a <strong>supervision loss</strong> (sometimes &quot;missing&quot; or &quot;check&quot;) trouble for that zone.</p>\n<ul><li>Supervision windows vary by panel and listing, commonly a few hours up to 24 hours</li><li>Some devices (like handheld remotes and panic pendants) are usually enrolled <strong>unsupervised</strong> because they leave the building</li><li>Fire and life-safety wireless devices must be supervised</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"5.3","title":"Enrolling sensors","html":"<p>General steps (exact keystrokes vary by panel):</p>\n<ol><li>Enter installer programming.</li><li>Choose the zone number and set its zone type and attributes.</li><li>Enroll the sensor's serial: either type it in, or put the panel in learn mode and trip the sensor (or pull its tamper) to transmit.</li><li>Set which loop/input of the sensor to use if the device has more than one.</li><li>Exit programming and <strong>test</strong> the zone.</li></ol>\n<p>Record every serial number and location on the zone list.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: your process for keeping serials straight when enrolling 20+ sensors.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"5.4","title":"Range and site survey","html":"<p>Wireless range on the spec sheet is measured in open air. Real buildings cut it down.</p>\n<p><strong>Things that kill signal</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Metal: steel doors and frames, metal siding, foil-backed insulation, mirrors, appliances, ductwork</li><li>Concrete and brick, especially with rebar</li><li>Water: aquariums, water heaters, people in the way</li><li>Distance and multiple walls</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Before you mount</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Hold the sensor where it'll go and trip it while watching the panel's <strong>signal strength</strong> reading (most panels show this in a test mode).</li><li>Aim for the &quot;good&quot; or better range the manufacturer recommends; marginal signal will become a supervision trouble later.</li><li>Move the receiver to a central spot if many sensors are weak, or add a <strong>repeater</strong> if the system supports one.</li><li>Don't mount the panel or receiver inside a metal enclosure or right next to the electrical panel.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>RF jamming:</strong> many panels detect a jammer and can report it. If you get jam troubles, look for other wireless equipment on nearby frequencies.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.5","title":"Batteries","html":"<ul><li>Most sensors use lithium batteries rated for roughly 3 to 5 years, but heavy-traffic doors and frequently triggered motions drain faster.</li><li>The panel shows a <strong>low battery</strong> trouble for the sensor before it dies, usually with weeks of warning.</li><li>Use the <strong>exact</strong> battery type the manufacturer lists. A wrong battery may fit but have the wrong voltage or chemistry.</li><li>Note battery replacement dates on the service record. Some companies replace all sensor batteries on a schedule.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"5.6","title":"Hybrid systems","html":"<p>A <strong>hybrid</strong> system combines a hardwired panel with a wireless receiver. This is common when:</p>\n<ul><li>Existing wired zones work fine, but new openings are hard to wire</li><li>Adding sensors to a finished home</li><li>Upgrading an older wired system without rewiring</li></ul>\n<p>Plan zone numbers so wired and wireless zones are clearly separated on the zone list.</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What does a wireless sensor's serial number do?","What's the difference between one-way and two-way wireless?","What trouble shows when the panel stops hearing a sensor's check-in?","Why are handheld remotes usually unsupervised?","Name three materials that reduce wireless range.","What should you check before permanently mounting a wireless sensor?","What's a hybrid system?"],"answers":["It identifies that sensor so the panel can assign it to a zone","Two-way has the panel confirm and talk back; one-way only transmits","Supervision loss (missing/check)","They leave the building, so they'd constantly show missing","Any three from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/5/5.4\">Lesson 5.4</a>","Signal strength at that location","A hardwired panel with added wireless sensors through a receiver"],"keyHtml":"1) It identifies that sensor so the panel can assign it to a zone. 2) Two-way has the panel confirm and talk back; one-way only transmits. 3) Supervision loss (missing/check). 4) They leave the building, so they'd constantly show missing. 5) Any three from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/5/5.4\">Lesson 5.4</a>. 6) Signal strength at that location. 7) A hardwired panel with added wireless sensors through a receiver.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":6,"title":"Installation","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> survey a site, design a system, place every device correctly, and install the panel, keypads, sirens, and communicator.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"6.1","title":"Site survey","html":"<p>Walk the whole property with the customer before you design anything.</p>\n<p><strong>Ask the customer</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Who lives or works here? Pets (weight, do they climb)? Kids? Elderly?</li><li>Which door do you use most?</li><li>What are you most worried about? (Break-in while away, while sleeping, a specific valuables room, a detached garage)</li><li>Any previous break-ins or false alarm history?</li><li>Do you want smoke, CO, water, or cameras later?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Check the building</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Every exterior door, window, and basement or crawlspace access</li><li>Construction: drywall, plaster, block, finished vs unfinished basement and attic (this decides wired vs wireless)</li><li>Panel location options near an outlet</li><li>Cell signal strength at the panel location</li><li>Existing system or wiring you can reuse</li><li>Local alarm permit requirements (see <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/8\">Module 8</a>)</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"6.2","title":"Designing the system","html":"<p><strong>Layers of protection</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Perimeter:</strong> contacts on exterior doors and accessible windows; glass break where glass can be broken.</li><li><strong>Interior:</strong> motion detectors covering traffic paths (main hallway, stairs, rooms with valuables).</li><li><strong>Life safety:</strong> smoke, heat, CO if part of the job.</li><li><strong>Annunciation and communication:</strong> keypads, sirens, communicator.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Design rules of thumb</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Every exterior door gets a contact.</li><li>Ground-floor and accessible windows get contacts or glass break coverage.</li><li>Place at least one motion so an intruder can't get from any ground-floor opening to the bedrooms or valuables without crossing it.</li><li>Primary keypad within reach of the main entry door; second keypad by the master bedroom or garage entry as needed.</li><li>Group devices into zones so the central station report tells police where to go.</li></ul>\n<p>Write a <strong>zone list</strong> before you start: zone number, name, device type, zone type, wired or wireless.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"6.3","title":"Device placement quick rules","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Do</th><th>Don't</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Door contact</td><td>Mount on the latch side at the top of the door</td><td>Mount on the hinge side (smallest gap movement)</td></tr><tr><td>Window contact</td><td>Mount on the moving sash</td><td>Leave windows that open with no contact</td></tr><tr><td>PIR</td><td>Mount at the rated height, aimed across traffic</td><td>Aim at windows, heat sources, vents, fans</td></tr><tr><td>Glass break</td><td>Line of sight to the glass, within rated range</td><td>Mount on the same wall as the glass if the manufacturer prohibits it, or behind heavy drapes</td></tr><tr><td>Keypad</td><td>Inside, near entry, about 4 to 5 ft high</td><td>Visible through a window from outside</td></tr><tr><td>Interior siren</td><td>Central location, out of easy reach</td><td>Right next to the keypad where it's easily silenced</td></tr><tr><td>Exterior siren/strobe</td><td>High, hard to reach, visible from the street</td><td>Within easy reach</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","mins":2},{"id":"6.4","title":"Mounting the panel and power","html":"<ol><li>Mount the can on a solid surface at a comfortable working height, with room around it for wiring.</li><li>Bring all cables in neatly through knockouts. Use grommets or bushings.</li><li>Connect earth ground per the manual.</li><li>Run the transformer wire. Use the transformer the manual specifies, plugged into a non-switched outlet. Secure it with its retaining screw if it has one.</li><li>Land all zones, bus devices, and outputs before applying power.</li><li><strong>Power-up order:</strong> connect the battery first or AC first as the manual says. Many manuals say AC first, then battery. Follow your panel's instructions.</li><li>Verify voltage at aux and bell outputs under load.</li></ol>","mins":2},{"id":"6.5","title":"Keypads and sirens","html":"<ul><li>Address each keypad before or after landing it, as the manual says. Duplicate addresses cause bus trouble.</li><li>Confirm keypad backlight and display at the farthest keypad; low voltage there means a voltage drop problem.</li><li>Siren wiring is polarity-sensitive on most sirens: positive to bell +, negative to bell −.</li><li>Some panels supervise the bell circuit and need an EOL resistor on it.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"6.6","title":"Communicators","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cellular</strong></td><td>Works when phone/internet lines are cut; most common today</td><td>Needs a usable signal; network sunsets require upgrades</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IP (internet)</strong></td><td>Fast, low cost</td><td>Fails if the router or internet is down or the customer changes equipment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dual-path (cell + IP)</strong></td><td>Redundant; often required for commercial</td><td>More cost</td></tr><tr><td><strong>POTS phone line</strong></td><td>Legacy</td><td>Copper landlines are being retired in many areas</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Install steps</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Check signal strength at the panel location before you mount. Use the communicator's own signal readout.</li><li>If signal is weak, use a remote antenna or relocate the communicator (within the manufacturer's limits).</li><li>Activate the communicator on the provider's portal and link it to the central station account.</li><li>Send test signals and confirm with the central station that they arrived with the right account and zone.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: what you do when the only weak-signal spot is where the customer wants the panel.</p></aside>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Name four questions to ask the customer during the survey.","What are the three layers of intrusion protection?","Which side of a door should a contact go on?","Why shouldn't the keypad be visible through a window?","What should you check before mounting a cellular communicator?","Why is dual-path communication more reliable?","Why does a duplicate keypad address cause problems?"],"answers":["Any four from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/6/6.1\">Lesson 6.1</a>","Perimeter, interior, annunciation/communication (life safety as an add-on)","The latch side","An intruder could see codes being entered or system status","Signal strength at that location","If one path fails, the other still sends the signal","The panel can't tell the two keypads apart, causing communication errors"],"keyHtml":"1) Any four from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/6/6.1\">Lesson 6.1</a>. 2) Perimeter, interior, annunciation/communication (life safety as an add-on). 3) The latch side. 4) An intruder could see codes being entered or system status. 5) Signal strength at that location. 6) If one path fails, the other still sends the signal. 7) The panel can't tell the two keypads apart, causing communication errors.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":7,"title":"Programming and Commissioning","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> program zone types, delays, user codes, and central station reporting, then fully test the system and hand it off to the customer.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"7.1","title":"Zone types","html":"<p>Names vary by brand; the behavior is what matters.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Zone type</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Typical devices</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Entry/Exit (delay)</strong></td><td>Starts the entry delay when tripped while armed; ignored during exit delay</td><td>Main entry door, garage entry door</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Perimeter (instant)</strong></td><td>Alarms immediately when armed in Stay or Away</td><td>Other exterior doors, windows, glass break</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interior follower</strong></td><td>Instant, unless an entry/exit zone was tripped first, then it follows the entry delay. Bypassed in Stay</td><td>Motion and interior doors on the entry path</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interior instant</strong></td><td>Instant in Away; bypassed in Stay</td><td>Motion away from the entry path</td></tr><tr><td><strong>24-hour audible / silent</strong></td><td>Always active, armed or not</td><td>Panic, hold-up</td></tr><tr><td><strong>24-hour fire</strong></td><td>Always active, fire priority</td><td>Smoke, heat</td></tr><tr><td><strong>24-hour supervisory / auxiliary</strong></td><td>Always active, non-burglary</td><td>Water, low temperature</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Day zone / trouble by day</strong></td><td>Trouble beep when disarmed, alarm when armed</td><td>Emergency exit doors at a business</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Why &quot;interior follower&quot; matters:</strong> when the homeowner comes in through the front door and walks past the hallway motion, the motion must give them the entry delay. If you program that motion as interior instant, it alarms before they reach the keypad.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"7.2","title":"Entry and exit delays","html":"<ul><li><strong>Exit delay:</strong> time after arming to leave. <strong>Entry delay:</strong> time after opening an entry door to disarm.</li><li>The ANSI/SIA CP-01 false alarm standard sets minimums and defaults for these: for example, a minimum exit delay of 45 seconds (default 60) and entry delay minimum of 30 seconds, with a combined entry delay plus abort window not to exceed 1 minute.</li><li>Set delays long enough for the customer's real path (garage door to keypad with groceries), but not so long that an intruder gets far.</li><li>Multiple entry delays are available on many panels (short for the front door, longer for the garage).</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"7.3","title":"User codes and authority levels","html":"<ul><li><strong>Installer code:</strong> your access to programming. Change it from the factory default on every install; leaving defaults is a security hole.</li><li><strong>Master code:</strong> the customer's top code; can add and delete users.</li><li><strong>User codes:</strong> for family members or employees; can be limited by partition, schedule, or function.</li><li><strong>Duress code:</strong> disarms normally but silently sends a duress signal.</li><li><strong>Temporary / one-time codes:</strong> for cleaners, contractors.</li></ul>\n<p>Teach the customer to give each person their own code so event history shows who armed and disarmed.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"7.4","title":"Central station setup","html":"<p>You need from the central station or your dealer portal:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Account number</strong> for the site</li><li><strong>Receiver phone number or IP/cellular path</strong> (often pre-set by the communicator provider)</li><li><strong>Reporting format:</strong> most panels today use <strong>Contact ID</strong> or <strong>SIA</strong> format</li></ul>\n<p>Then program:</p>\n<ul><li>Which events report (alarms, restores, troubles, openings/closings, tests)</li><li><strong>Periodic test</strong> timer (commonly every 24 hours, sometimes more often for commercial) so the central station knows the communicator is alive</li><li><strong>Abort window</strong> (also called communication delay) and <strong>cancel</strong> reporting per CP-01</li><li>Zone list sent to the central station with names, zone types, and the customer's call list</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"7.5","title":"Testing and commissioning","html":"<p><strong>1. Put the account on test</strong> with the central station first. Never trip a live system without doing this.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Walk test</strong> every zone. Most panels have a walk-test mode that chimes each zone as it trips.</p>\n<ul><li>Open every door and window with a contact.</li><li>Walk every motion pattern from all likely approaches.</li><li>Use the simulator on every glass break.</li><li>Pull every tamper you can.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>3. Test communication.</strong> Arm the system, trip a zone, let it alarm, and confirm with the central station that they received the alarm with the right zone number and name. Test troubles too (AC loss, low battery if practical).</p>\n<p><strong>4. Test power.</strong> Unplug AC and confirm the system runs on battery and reports AC loss. Check battery voltage under load.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Take the account off test</strong>, confirming with the operator.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Document:</strong> zone list, device locations, serial numbers, test results, battery install date, communicator ID.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"7.6","title":"Customer training and handoff","html":"<p>A trained customer is the best way to prevent false alarms.</p>\n<p>Cover, with the customer doing it themselves:</p>\n<ul><li>Arming Away, Stay, and Night, and what each does</li><li>Disarming, and what to do if they set it off by mistake (disarm, then answer the central station's call and give their passcode)</li><li>Their <strong>verbal passcode</strong> for the central station</li><li>Bypassing a zone, and why not to leave windows bypassed forever</li><li>What troubles look like and who to call</li><li>How to use the phone app if there is one</li><li>Panic buttons and what they do</li><li>Keeping the call list up to date</li></ul>\n<p>Leave a printed or digital quick-start sheet and the zone list.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the one thing customers always forget after handoff.</p></aside>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Why should hallway motion be programmed as interior follower and not interior instant?","What does a duress code do?","Why must you change the installer code from the default?","What does a periodic test signal tell the central station?","What's the first thing you do before walk-testing a monitored system?","Name four things the customer should be able to do before you leave."],"answers":["So it follows the entry delay when the user walks in through the entry door","Disarms normally while silently sending a duress signal","Defaults are publicly known and anyone could reprogram the system","That the communicator and path are working","Put the account on test with the central station","Any four from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/7/7.6\">Lesson 7.6</a>"],"keyHtml":"1) So it follows the entry delay when the user walks in through the entry door. 2) Disarms normally while silently sending a duress signal. 3) Defaults are publicly known and anyone could reprogram the system. 4) That the communicator and path are working. 5) Put the account on test with the central station. 6) Any four from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/7/7.6\">Lesson 7.6</a>.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":8,"title":"Codes, Standards, and False Alarm Reduction","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> explain who sets the rules for an alarm install, name the key standards, use false alarm reduction features, and document a job properly.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout\"><p>These lessons give an overview. Codes are adopted and amended locally, and editions change. Always confirm requirements with your company, the AHJ, and the current adopted edition.</p></aside>","lessons":[{"id":"8.1","title":"The AHJ","html":"<p>The <strong>Authority Having Jurisdiction (AHJ)</strong> is the office or person who enforces the rules where you're working: often a city or county building department, the fire marshal, or the police department's alarm unit.</p>\n<p>The AHJ decides:</p>\n<ul><li>Whether a permit is required for the install</li><li>Which code editions apply</li><li>Whether an inspection is required</li><li>Alarm registration and false alarm fines</li></ul>\n<p>When in doubt, call the AHJ before the job.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.2","title":"Licensing, permits, and alarm ordinances","html":"<ul><li>Many states and cities require <strong>alarm company and/or technician licenses</strong>, sometimes with background checks and continuing education. Know what your state requires before you work.</li><li>Many cities require the <strong>customer to register</strong> the alarm (alarm permit) before police will respond.</li><li><strong>Alarm ordinances</strong> often set false alarm fees, bell cutoff time limits for exterior sirens, and verification requirements (such as Enhanced Call Verification or video/audio verified response).</li><li>Some jurisdictions use <strong>verified response</strong>: police won't respond to a burglar alarm unless it's verified by video, audio, or a person on site.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"8.3","title":"Key standards","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>What it covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ANSI/SIA CP-01</strong></td><td>Control panel features for false alarm reduction (delays, abort window, swinger shutdown, exit error, and more)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 70 (National Electrical Code)</strong></td><td>Wiring rules, including power-limited (Class 2) circuits and cable ratings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 731</strong></td><td>Installation of electronic premises security systems</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 72</strong></td><td>Fire alarm and household fire warning equipment (applies when you install smoke/heat/CO on a panel)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UL 681</strong></td><td>Installation and classification of burglar alarm systems</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UL 1023</strong></td><td>Household burglar alarm system units</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UL 1610 / UL 1076</strong></td><td>Central station and proprietary burglar alarm units</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UL 827</strong></td><td>Central station alarm services</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>UL certificated systems:</strong> some commercial customers (often due to insurance) need a UL certificated burglar alarm system. That means a listed alarm company, listed equipment, installation to UL 681, and a certificate issued for the specific system. This goes beyond v1 but you should know it exists and when to call your supervisor.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.4","title":"False alarm reduction","html":"<p>Most alarm dispatches are false alarms, and most false alarms are <strong>user error</strong>, followed by equipment and installation problems. Every one costs the customer fines and police time.</p>\n<p><strong>CP-01 panel features</strong> (typical; check the panel and standard for exact defaults):</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Exit delay progress / annunciation</strong></td><td>Keypad beeps during exit delay, faster near the end</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exit error</strong></td><td>If an entry door opens and closes during exit delay but isn't closed when the delay ends, the panel goes into entry delay and sends an exit error code instead of an instant alarm</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Auto Stay</strong></td><td>If armed Away but nobody leaves through an entry door, the panel automatically arms Stay</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Abort window</strong></td><td>Holds the alarm signal briefly so a quick disarm cancels it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cancel report</strong></td><td>If disarmed after the signal was sent, sends a cancel message</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Swinger shutdown</strong></td><td>Limits how many times one zone can trip in an armed period</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cross-zoning</strong></td><td>Requires two zones to trip within a time window before alarming (use with care)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Recent closing</strong></td><td>Reports when an alarm happens shortly after arming</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Duress</strong></td><td>Silent signal with a special code</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Installer practices that prevent false alarms</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Correct PIR placement and pet settings (<a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/3\">Module 3</a>)</li><li>Tight contact alignment and correct gaps</li><li>Interior follower zones on the entry path (<a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/7\">Module 7</a>)</li><li>Batteries replaced on schedule</li><li>Thorough customer training (<a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/7\">Module 7</a>)</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"8.5","title":"Documentation","html":"<p>Leave and keep:</p>\n<ul><li>Zone list with device type, location, zone type, wired/wireless, serial numbers</li><li>Wiring diagram or panel drawing for anything non-obvious</li><li>Programming summary or a saved programming file (stored securely)</li><li>Test results and central station confirmation</li><li>Battery install dates</li><li>Permit number and inspection results, if any</li><li>Signed customer acceptance and training acknowledgment</li></ul>\n<p>Good documentation saves the next service call, and protects you and your company if something goes wrong.</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What does AHJ stand for, and what does it decide?","What does verified response mean?","Which standard covers control panel features for false alarm reduction?","What's an abort window?","What does swinger shutdown prevent?","What's the most common cause of false alarms?","Name four items to document on every job."],"answers":["Authority Having Jurisdiction; permits, applicable codes, inspections, registration and fines","Police respond only if the alarm is verified by video, audio, or a person","ANSI/SIA CP-01","A short delay before reporting so a quick disarm cancels the signal","One faulty zone sending repeated alarms in a single armed period","User error","Any four from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/8/8.5\">Lesson 8.5</a>"],"keyHtml":"1) Authority Having Jurisdiction; permits, applicable codes, inspections, registration and fines. 2) Police respond only if the alarm is verified by video, audio, or a person. 3) ANSI/SIA CP-01. 4) A short delay before reporting so a quick disarm cancels the signal. 5) One faulty zone sending repeated alarms in a single armed period. 6) User error. 7) Any four from <a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/8/8.5\">Lesson 8.5</a>.","noteHtml":""}}]},"reference":{"introHtml":"<p>Quick cards for the Reference tab. Each <code>##</code> section is one card. Values reviewed and signed off by David on 2026-10-04; panel-specific values always defer to the installation manual.</p>","cards":[{"id":"zone-wiring-at-a-glance","title":"Zone wiring at a glance","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>NC, single EOL (series contacts, resistor at last device)\nPANEL Z ──[C1]──[C2]──[C3]──┐\n                            [EOL]\nPANEL COM ──────────────────┘\nNormal = EOL   Open/cut = ALARM   Short = trouble/alarm (panel)\n\nNO, single EOL (parallel devices, resistor across at last device)\nPANEL Z ───┬────┬────┬──────┐\n          [D1] [D2] [D3]  [EOL]\nPANEL COM ─┴────┴────┴──────┘\nNormal = EOL   Device closes (short) = ALARM   Cut = TROUBLE\n\nDouble EOL (per device: R in series, R across the contact)\nPANEL Z ──[R]──┬─[C]─┐\n               └─[R]─┘\nPANEL COM ─────────────\nClosed = R   Open = 2R (ALARM)   Cut = open (TAMPER)   Short = 0 (FAULT)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"meter-readings-on-a-zone","title":"Meter readings on a zone","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Reading (wires off the panel)</th><th>Single EOL NC</th><th>Single EOL NO</th><th>DEOL (equal R)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>≈ EOL value</td><td>Normal</td><td>Normal</td><td>Normal (contact closed)</td></tr><tr><td>≈ 2 × EOL</td><td>Two resistors in series (miswire)</td><td>Miswire</td><td>Alarm (contact open)</td></tr><tr><td>≈ ½ × EOL</td><td>Two resistors in parallel (miswire)</td><td>Two EOLs installed</td><td>Miswire</td></tr><tr><td>OL / open</td><td>Device open or wire cut</td><td>Wire cut</td><td>Wire cut / tamper</td></tr><tr><td>≈ 0 Ω</td><td>Short</td><td>Device closed or short</td><td>Short</td></tr><tr><td>Any reading to earth ground</td><td>Ground fault</td><td>Ground fault</td><td>Ground fault</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tolerance: within about ±10% of the expected value is normal. Higher than expected, with no open, usually means a corroded or loose splice.</p>"},{"id":"common-eol-resistor-values","title":"Common EOL resistor values","html":"<p>Always use the value the panel manual calls for. Common values seen in the field:</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Color bands (4-band, 5% gold)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 kΩ</td><td>Brown, Black, Red, Gold</td></tr><tr><td>2 kΩ</td><td>Red, Black, Red, Gold</td></tr><tr><td>2.2 kΩ</td><td>Red, Red, Red, Gold</td></tr><tr><td>4.7 kΩ</td><td>Yellow, Violet, Red, Gold</td></tr><tr><td>5.6 kΩ</td><td>Green, Blue, Red, Gold</td></tr><tr><td>10 kΩ</td><td>Brown, Black, Orange, Gold</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Color code: Black 0, Brown 1, Red 2, Orange 3, Yellow 4, Green 5, Blue 6, Violet 7, Gray 8, White 9. Bands 1 and 2 are digits, band 3 is the number of zeros, band 4 is tolerance.</p>"},{"id":"copper-wire-resistance","title":"Copper wire resistance","html":"<p>Approximate resistance of solid copper at 68 °F (20 °C), per conductor:</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>AWG</th><th>Ω per 1,000 ft</th><th>Ω per foot</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>22</td><td>16.1</td><td>0.0161</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>10.2</td><td>0.0102</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>6.39</td><td>0.00639</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>4.02</td><td>0.00402</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>2.53</td><td>0.00253</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>1.59</td><td>0.00159</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Voltage drop = 2 × one-way length (ft) × current (A) × Ω per foot.</strong> The 2 accounts for the wire out and back. Use the <a href=\"#/intrusion/calculators/drop\">Voltage Drop calculator</a>.</p>"},{"id":"battery-standby-formula","title":"Battery standby formula","html":"<p><strong>Required Ah = [(total standby current in A × standby hours) + (total alarm current in A × alarm hours)] × 1.2 safety factor</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Alarm minutes ÷ 60 = alarm hours (for example 4 min = 0.067 h)</li><li>Round up to the next standard battery size (common sizes 4, 5, 7, 8, 12, 18 Ah)</li><li>Commonly cited standby requirements: about 4 hours for UL residential burglary, 24 hours for UL commercial/certificated burglary, 24 hours for fire</li><li>Check the panel's maximum battery size and charger capability</li></ul>\n<p>Use the <a href=\"#/intrusion/calculators/battery\">Battery Standby calculator</a>.</p>"},{"id":"zone-type-cheat-sheet","title":"Zone type cheat sheet","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Armed Away</th><th>Armed Stay</th><th>Disarmed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry/Exit</td><td>Delay</td><td>Delay</td><td>Chime (if set)</td></tr><tr><td>Perimeter</td><td>Instant</td><td>Instant</td><td>Chime (if set)</td></tr><tr><td>Interior follower</td><td>Instant, or delay after entry zone</td><td>Bypassed</td><td>Ignored</td></tr><tr><td>Interior instant</td><td>Instant</td><td>Bypassed</td><td>Ignored</td></tr><tr><td>24-hour panic/hold-up</td><td>Alarm</td><td>Alarm</td><td>Alarm</td></tr><tr><td>24-hour fire</td><td>Fire alarm</td><td>Fire alarm</td><td>Fire alarm</td></tr><tr><td>24-hour supervisory</td><td>Report</td><td>Report</td><td>Report</td></tr><tr><td>Day zone</td><td>Alarm</td><td>Alarm</td><td>Trouble beep</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"device-placement-quick-rules","title":"Device placement quick rules","html":"<ul><li><strong>Door contact:</strong> latch side, top of door. Steel doors cut gap; use steel-rated contacts.</li><li><strong>Overhead door contact:</strong> floor mount at the leading edge or track mount; armored cable.</li><li><strong>PIR:</strong> rated height (commonly 7 to 7.5 ft), aimed across traffic. Not at heat, vents, sunlit windows, fans.</li><li><strong>Pet-immune PIR:</strong> exact rated height and orientation; no furniture or stairs pets can climb in view.</li><li><strong>Dual-tech:</strong> turn microwave range down so it doesn't see through walls or windows.</li><li><strong>Glass break:</strong> clear line of sight, within rated range (commonly up to about 20 to 25 ft); test with the listed simulator.</li><li><strong>Keypad:</strong> inside near entry, not visible from outside.</li><li><strong>Siren:</strong> central, out of reach. Exterior: high and visible.</li><li><strong>Wireless:</strong> check signal strength before mounting; avoid metal.</li></ul>"},{"id":"contact-id-event-codes-common","title":"Contact ID event codes (common)","html":"<p>Contact ID message: <code>ACCT 18 Q EEE GG ZZZ</code></p>\n<ul><li>ACCT = 4-digit account, 18 = message type, Q = qualifier (1 = new event/opening, 3 = restore/closing), EEE = event code, GG = partition, ZZZ = zone or user number</li></ul>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Event</th><th></th><th>Code</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100</td><td>Medical</td><td></td><td>301</td><td>AC loss</td></tr><tr><td>110</td><td>Fire</td><td></td><td>302</td><td>Low system battery</td></tr><tr><td>120</td><td>Panic</td><td></td><td>344</td><td>RF receiver jam</td></tr><tr><td>121</td><td>Duress</td><td></td><td>350</td><td>Communication trouble</td></tr><tr><td>122</td><td>Silent panic</td><td></td><td>373</td><td>Fire trouble</td></tr><tr><td>130</td><td>Burglary</td><td></td><td>380</td><td>Sensor trouble</td></tr><tr><td>131</td><td>Perimeter burglary</td><td></td><td>381</td><td>Loss of RF supervision</td></tr><tr><td>132</td><td>Interior burglary</td><td></td><td>383</td><td>Sensor tamper</td></tr><tr><td>134</td><td>Entry/exit burglary</td><td></td><td>384</td><td>RF low battery</td></tr><tr><td>137</td><td>Tamper</td><td></td><td>401</td><td>Open/close by user</td></tr><tr><td>139</td><td>Verified intrusion</td><td></td><td>406</td><td>Cancel</td></tr><tr><td>145</td><td>Expansion module tamper</td><td></td><td>570</td><td>Zone bypass</td></tr><tr><td>150</td><td>24-hour non-burglary</td><td></td><td>602</td><td>Periodic test</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"communicator-quick-facts","title":"Communicator quick facts","html":"<ul><li>Check cell signal at the panel before mounting; use the communicator's own signal readout.</li><li>Weak signal: remote antenna, relocate within manufacturer limits, or dual-path.</li><li>After install: activate on the provider portal, link to central station account, send test signals, confirm account and zone at the station.</li><li>Periodic test commonly every 24 h; more often for commercial or certificated systems.</li></ul>"},{"id":"before-you-leave-the-job","title":"Before you leave the job","html":"<ul class=\"checklist\"><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Account taken off test, confirmed with the operator</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>All zones walk-tested and reported correctly</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>AC loss and battery verified</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Installer code changed from default</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Customer trained and knows their passcode</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Zone list and documentation left in panel and uploaded</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Panel can closed and tamper restored</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Work area cleaned</span></label></li></ul>"},{"id":"glossary","title":"Glossary","html":"<ul><li><strong>AHJ:</strong> Authority Having Jurisdiction; the office that enforces codes where you work.</li><li><strong>Abort window:</strong> delay before an alarm is reported so a quick disarm cancels it.</li><li><strong>Aux power:</strong> the panel's DC output for powering devices.</li><li><strong>Bus:</strong> shared 4-wire power and data connection for keypads and modules.</li><li><strong>Bypass:</strong> temporarily ignore a zone.</li><li><strong>Central station:</strong> monitoring center that receives signals and dispatches.</li><li><strong>Contact ID / SIA:</strong> common formats for reporting events to the central station.</li><li><strong>CP-01:</strong> ANSI/SIA standard for false alarm reduction features in control panels.</li><li><strong>DEOL:</strong> double end-of-line; two resistors per zone to tell alarm from tamper.</li><li><strong>ECV:</strong> Enhanced Call Verification; two calls to verify before dispatch.</li><li><strong>EOL:</strong> end-of-line resistor; lets the panel supervise the zone wiring.</li><li><strong>NC / NO:</strong> normally closed / normally open.</li><li><strong>Partition:</strong> a group of zones armed and disarmed as its own system.</li><li><strong>PIR:</strong> passive infrared motion detector.</li><li><strong>Supervision:</strong> the panel checking that devices and paths are working.</li><li><strong>Swinger shutdown:</strong> limit on repeated alarms from one zone.</li><li><strong>Tamper:</strong> a switch or circuit that detects a device or cover being opened.</li><li><strong>Zone:</strong> one input the panel monitors.</li></ul>"}]},"troubleshoot":{"introHtml":"<p>One guide per common service call, for the Troubleshoot tab. Each guide is a step-by-step flowchart: do the step, then follow the result. Always put the account <strong>on test</strong> with the central station before you trip anything, and take it off test when you're done.</p>","guides":[{"num":1,"title":"Zone shows trouble or fault","symptomHtml":"Keypad shows a trouble, fault, or &quot;check&quot; on a hardwired zone.","html":"<ol><li>Note the zone number and look it up on the zone list. Is it a wireless zone? → Go to <a href=\"#/intrusion/troubleshoot/7\">Guide 7</a>.</li><li>Look at the device. Is it physically damaged, missing, or its cover off? → Repair or replace; restore the tamper.</li><li>Remove the zone wires from the panel and meter them (Reference: <a href=\"#/intrusion/reference/meter-readings-on-a-zone\"><em>Meter readings on a zone</em></a>).<ul><li>Reads normal EOL value → wiring is good. Check the panel terminal (loose screw, corrosion) and the zone's programmed EOL setting. Swap the wires to a known good zone to see if the problem follows the wire or stays with the panel input.</li><li>Reads 0 Ω (short) → go to step 4.</li><li>Reads open → go to step 5.</li><li>Reads a wrong value → wrong or extra resistor, or a high-resistance splice. Find the EOL and check every splice.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Short:</strong> look for a staple through the cable, a pinched wire behind trim, a crushed cable in a door frame, or water in a splice. Split the run in half at an accessible device and test each half to narrow it down.</li><li><strong>Open:</strong> check each contact with its magnet in place (it should read closed). Check splices and the EOL itself. Split the run at accessible points to narrow it down.</li><li>After repair, meter again, re-land, confirm the zone restores, and walk-test it.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: fastest way you've found to locate a short in a finished wall.</p></aside>"},{"num":2,"title":"Zone won't restore / system won't arm","symptomHtml":"Keypad shows &quot;not ready&quot; or a zone open, and the customer can't arm.","html":"<ol><li>Check the keypad for which zone is open. Is that door or window actually open? → Close it.</li><li>Close the opening. Does the zone still show open?<ul><li>Check magnet alignment and gap. Has the door sagged or the window shifted? Realign or use a wide-gap contact.</li><li>Check for a missing magnet (painted over, knocked off, removed during window replacement).</li></ul></li><li>If it's a motion, is something moving in its view (fan, curtains, pet, balloon)? The zone may restore after a few seconds of no motion.</li><li>Still open: meter the zone (<a href=\"#/intrusion/troubleshoot/1\">Guide 1</a>, step 3).</li><li><strong>Short-term fix for the customer:</strong> bypass the zone so they can arm, and tell them clearly that the zone is unprotected until it's repaired. Note it on the ticket.</li></ol>"},{"num":3,"title":"False alarms from a specific zone","symptomHtml":"The central station or customer reports repeated alarms from one zone with no break-in.","html":"<ol><li>Pull the event history: what times, what arming mode, and was it the same zone each time?<ul><li>Alarms right after arming or right at entry → likely user error or delay settings. Check entry/exit times, check that motions on the entry path are interior follower (<a href=\"#/intrusion/learn/7\">Module 7</a>), retrain the customer.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Contact:</strong> check gap and alignment. Does the zone open when you push or rattle the door? Wind and loose doors cause this. Tighten, realign, or replace with a wide-gap contact.</li><li><strong>Motion:</strong> look for heat sources, HVAC vents, sunlight, curtains, fans, new furniture pets can climb, or a recent change in pets. Check mounting height and pet setting. Consider dual-tech or relocating.</li><li><strong>Glass break:</strong> check sensitivity, nearby noise sources (dishes, dog barking, TV), and whether it was moved closer to a noise source.</li><li><strong>Wiring:</strong> an intermittent open causes alarms with nothing visible. Meter the zone while wiggling the cable at the device, splices, and panel. Look for corroded splices in damp areas.</li><li><strong>Wireless:</strong> check signal strength and battery. Weak signal can cause missed restores. See <a href=\"#/intrusion/troubleshoot/7\">Guide 7</a>.</li><li>Set or verify <strong>swinger shutdown</strong> so one bad zone can't keep dispatching while you fix it.</li><li>Document what you found and changed.</li></ol>"},{"num":4,"title":"Low battery or AC loss","symptomHtml":"Keypad shows low battery, battery trouble, AC loss, or power trouble.","html":"<ol><li><strong>AC loss first:</strong> is the transformer plugged in? Is the outlet live (on a switch, tripped GFCI, or tripped breaker)? Reconnect or have the customer reset the breaker or GFCI.</li><li>Meter AC at the panel's AC terminals (AC volts). Expect about the transformer's rated output. Low or zero with the transformer plugged in → check the wire run, then the transformer.</li><li><strong>Battery:</strong> disconnect the battery and meter it.<ul><li>A 12 V sealed lead-acid battery at roughly 12.6 V or more is charged; much lower than 12 V usually means it's weak or dead. A battery reading OK unloaded can still fail under load.</li><li>Check the battery date. Many companies replace panel batteries every 3 to 5 years.</li></ul></li><li>Check charging voltage at the panel's battery leads with the battery disconnected (commonly around 13.5 to 13.8 VDC). Low charge voltage points to a panel or transformer problem.</li><li>Replace the battery if it's old or fails a load test. Give it time to charge; many panels run a battery test periodically and may show the trouble until the next test or a manual test clears it.</li><li>Recurring low battery with a new battery: the standby load may be too high. Measure total aux current and run the <a href=\"#/intrusion/calculators/battery\">Battery Standby calculator</a>. Add a power supply if needed.</li></ol>"},{"num":5,"title":"Keypad blank, dead, or \"system busy\"","symptomHtml":"Keypad display is blank, frozen, or shows busy/not available.","html":"<ol><li>Are all keypads affected, or just one?<ul><li><strong>All keypads blank</strong> → panel power problem. Check AC and battery (<a href=\"#/intrusion/troubleshoot/4\">Guide 4</a>). Check the aux output fuse or PTC; a short on aux can shut down every keypad. Disconnect aux devices one group at a time to find the short.</li><li><strong>One keypad</strong> → continue.</li></ul></li><li>Meter voltage at the keypad's power terminals. Expect near panel aux voltage.<ul><li>Low voltage → voltage drop (long run or thin wire) or a bad splice. Use the <a href=\"#/intrusion/calculators/drop\">Voltage Drop calculator</a>; upgrade wire or add a power supply.</li><li>Zero → open in the power wires. Check splices and terminals.</li></ul></li><li>Check the keypad's address. A duplicate or out-of-range address causes errors or no display.</li><li><strong>&quot;Busy&quot; or &quot;system not available&quot;</strong> can also mean another keypad or software is in programming mode, or the panel is still powering up. Wait a minute and exit programming at the other keypad or software.</li><li>Swap in a known good keypad at that location to tell keypad failure from wiring.</li></ol>"},{"num":6,"title":"Bus or keypad communication errors","symptomHtml":"Module or keypad communication failure, bus fault, or supervision loss on a bus device.","html":"<ol><li>Check the keypad or event history for which address is failing.</li><li>Check that the device's address is set correctly and isn't duplicated.</li><li>Meter power at the device under load.</li><li>Check the data wires: correct terminals (wire colors vary by brand), tight connections, no reversed data lines.</li><li>Check bus length and topology against the manual. Long runs or a mix of star and daisy chain can exceed limits.</li><li>Check for interference: bus cable running alongside AC power, fluorescent ballasts, or motors.</li><li>Disconnect bus devices one at a time (power down first, if the manual says so) to find a device that's dragging the bus down.</li><li>After a fix, many panels need the bus devices re-enrolled or supervision reset.</li></ol>"},{"num":7,"title":"Wireless sensor supervision loss","symptomHtml":"A wireless zone shows missing, supervision, or check trouble.","html":"<ol><li>Is the sensor still there and in one piece? Customers sometimes remove sensors during painting or window replacement.</li><li>Check the sensor battery. Replace with the exact type the manufacturer lists.</li><li>Trip the sensor and watch signal strength in test mode.<ul><li>Weak → something changed: new metal (appliance, mirror, foil insulation, metal blinds), a moved receiver, or a remodel. Relocate the sensor or receiver, or add a repeater.</li><li>No signal at all → confirm the serial number is enrolled to that zone. Re-enroll if needed.</li></ul></li><li>Check the receiver: power, antennas, and location (not inside a metal can or next to the electrical panel).</li><li>If several sensors drop at once, suspect the receiver, RF jamming, or interference from new wireless equipment.</li><li>Clear the trouble per the panel's procedure and confirm the sensor checks in.</li></ol>"},{"num":8,"title":"Failure to communicate with the central station","symptomHtml":"Panel shows communication failure, or the central station reports a missed periodic test or no signals.","html":"<ol><li>Ask the central station what they last received and when.</li><li><strong>Cellular:</strong> check signal strength at the communicator. Has something blocked it (a new metal door, panel moved)? Is the communicator activated and the account in good standing on the provider portal? Is it on a network that's been retired? → Remote antenna, relocate, or upgrade the communicator.</li><li><strong>IP:</strong> is the customer's internet up? Did they change the router or internet provider? Check the Ethernet cable and link lights. The panel may need a new IP setting or DHCP.</li><li><strong>Phone line (POTS):</strong> is there dial tone? Many landlines have been replaced by VoIP or fiber that may not pass alarm signals reliably. Recommend cellular.</li><li>Check programming: account number, receiver path, and reporting format match what the central station expects.</li><li>Send a test signal and confirm with the operator that it arrived with the right account.</li></ol>"},{"num":9,"title":"Siren not sounding, or won't shut off","symptomHtml":"No sound on alarm, or the siren keeps sounding after disarm.","html":"<p><strong>No sound</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Check bell cutoff and siren programming (some installs or partitions are set to silent).</li><li>Meter the bell output during an alarm (on test with the central station). Expect about panel DC voltage.<ul><li>Voltage present → wiring to the siren or the siren itself. Check polarity, splices, and the siren.</li><li>No voltage → check the bell fuse or PTC, the bell supervision EOL if the panel needs one, and the bell output programming.</li></ul></li><li>Check total siren current against the bell output rating.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Won't shut off</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Disarm with a valid code. Still sounding? Check whether it's a different device (a smoke's built-in sounder, a separate system).</li><li>A 24-hour zone (fire, panic, tamper) may still be active. Clear the cause, then reset.</li><li>A tamper on an exterior siren box can keep it sounding; check the tamper and cover.</li><li>If you must silence it while troubleshooting, disconnect the siren wires at the panel and the self-contained siren's battery, and note it on the ticket.</li></ol>"},{"num":10,"title":"Tamper troubles","symptomHtml":"Tamper trouble on a zone, keypad, module, siren, or panel.","html":"<ol><li>Which device? Check the keypad or event history.</li><li><strong>Panel or module cover:</strong> is the door closed and the tamper switch engaged?</li><li><strong>Wireless sensor:</strong> is the cover snapped on fully? Was the sensor pulled off the wall?</li><li><strong>DEOL zone tamper:</strong> the zone wiring reads open. Meter it (<a href=\"#/intrusion/troubleshoot/1\">Guide 1</a>). Check that both resistors are installed and correct.</li><li><strong>Siren tamper:</strong> check the cover and tamper loop.</li><li>Clear the tamper per the panel; some need a code or a reset after the device restores.</li></ol>"},{"num":11,"title":"Motion detector not triggering or over-triggering","symptomHtml":"Walk test doesn't trip the motion, or it trips with nothing there.","html":"<p><strong>Not triggering</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Is the LED enabled for testing? (Many detectors disable it after setup.)</li><li>Meter power at the detector. Low or none → aux wiring or voltage drop.</li><li>Is it mounted at the rated height and aimed across the traffic path? Walking straight at a PIR is the weakest detection direction.</li><li>Is something blocking it (new shelving, a plant)?</li><li>Check jumper or DIP settings (pulse count, sensitivity, pet mode).</li><li>Meter the alarm contacts while walking in front of it. They should open. No change → replace the detector.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Over-triggering</strong> → see <a href=\"#/intrusion/troubleshoot/3\">Guide 3</a>, step 3.</p>"},{"num":12,"title":"Ground fault","symptomHtml":"Panel shows a ground fault trouble.","html":"<ol><li>Confirm the panel's earth ground connection is correct.</li><li>Disconnect zones and outputs one at a time (or in halves), clearing the trouble between each, until the ground fault clears. The last thing you removed has the fault.</li><li>Meter each conductor of that circuit to earth ground. Any reading means a wire is touching ground.</li><li>Look for a wire touching a metal box, a staple through a cable into a metal stud or conduit, water in an outdoor splice, or an outdoor device with damaged cable.</li><li>Repair, re-land, and confirm the trouble clears.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<p><em>These guides are a training aid. They don't replace the panel's installation manual, applicable codes, or your company's procedures.</em></p>"}]},"search":[{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.1 Voltage, current, and resistance","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.1","text":"Every alarm circuit comes down to three numbers. Term Symbol Unit Think of it as Voltage V (or E) volts (V) The push. How hard electricity is being pushed through the wire. Current I amps (A), milliamps (mA) The flow. How much electricity is actually moving. Resistance R ohms (Ω) The restriction. How hard the path is to push through. 1 amp = 1,000 milliamps. Alarm devices are usually rated in mA (a contact draws nothing, a motion detector draws roughly 10 to 30 mA, a keypad roughly 30 to 150 mA). Always check the device's spec sheet. Ohm's law V = I × R Cover the one you want to find: - Voltage = Current × Resistance - Current = Voltage ÷ Resistance - Resistance = Voltage ÷ Current Power: P = V × I, measured in watts (W). Example. A siren draws 0.5 A on a 12 V circuit. Power = 12 × 0.5 = 6 W. Example. A zone has a 2,200 Ω (2.2 kΩ) end-of-line resistor and the panel puts about 5 V on the loop. Current = 5 ÷ 2,200 ≈ 0.0023 A, or 2.3 mA. This tiny current is how the panel \"watches\" the zone. AC vs DC - AC (alternating current) reverses direction many times a second. The transformer that plugs into the wall puts out low-voltage AC (commonly 16.5 VAC or 18 VAC) to the panel. - DC (direct current) flows one way. The panel converts the AC to DC (usually about 12 to 13.8 VDC) to run devices and charge the battery. When you meter a panel's AC input terminals, set the meter to AC volts. Everywhere else in the system (aux power, bell output, battery) is DC."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.2 Series and parallel","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.2","text":"Series: devices connected end to end, one path. The same current flows through all of them. If any one opens, the whole circuit opens. - This is how normally closed door contacts are wired on one zone: open any door and the loop breaks. Parallel: devices connected across the same two wires, several paths. Each device gets the same voltage. If one opens, the others keep working. - This is how you power several motion detectors from aux power: each gets 12 V, and their currents add up. - This is how normally open devices (like some panic buttons) are wired on one zone: close any one and the loop shorts. Resistors in series add: 1 kΩ + 1 kΩ = 2 kΩ. Two equal resistors in parallel halve: 2 kΩ in parallel with 2 kΩ = 1 kΩ. You'll use those two rules constantly when you read a zone with a meter (see Module 4)."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.3 Class 2 and power-limited circuits","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.3","text":"Most intrusion wiring is Class 2 , a category in the National Electrical Code (NEC) for circuits whose power source is limited so they don't present a significant fire or shock risk. What that means for you: - Panels, transformers, and power supplies are listed as Class 2 sources, and their output is limited (typically no more than 100 VA for the low voltages we use). - Class 2 wiring has lighter rules than power wiring, but it still has rules: use listed cable (CL2, CL3, or a higher rating like CL2R/CL2P where required), keep it separated from line-voltage wiring, and support it properly. - Never run Class 2 cable in the same raceway or box as 120 V wiring unless a listed barrier separates them. - Plenum spaces (air-handling ceilings) require plenum-rated cable (CL2P or CMP). Local codes and the AHJ (authority having jurisdiction) can add requirements. See Module 8."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.4 Tools of the trade","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.4","text":"Must have - Digital multimeter (volts AC/DC, ohms, continuity, ideally DC milliamps) - Wire strippers sized for 22 to 14 AWG - Screwdrivers, including a small flat \"tweaker\" for terminal strips - Drill/driver, bits, and a long flex bit for fishing walls - Fish tape and glow rods - Cable staples/straps, wire nuts or butt connectors (gel-filled for damp areas) - Label maker or wire markers - Ladder (rated for your weight plus tools) - Flashlight or headlamp - Smartphone with installer app/manufacturer software Nice to have - Toner and probe (to trace unlabeled wires) - Inspection camera - Magnet set (to test contacts and find gap) - Glass break simulator - Walk-test light or second person on the phone - Cell signal meter Field tip (David to add): the one tool new techs forget, and why it matters."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.5 Job site safety","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.5","text":"- Ladders: three points of contact, never stand on the top two steps, set extension ladders at a 4:1 angle. - Attics and crawlspaces: step only on joists, watch for nails coming through the roof deck, wear a mask around insulation, and stay hydrated in hot attics. - Line voltage: you're a low-voltage tech. Don't open electrical panels or work on 120 V circuits unless you're licensed for it. When a transformer needs a dedicated outlet, that's an electrician's job. - Drilling: check for wires, pipes, and gas lines before you drill. Use an inspection camera or stud finder with AC detection. - Batteries: sealed lead-acid batteries can deliver a lot of current. Don't short the terminals with a tool. Disconnect the battery before working on the panel board. - Customer homes: shoe covers, drop cloths, clean up dust, ask before moving furniture. - Personal safety: in vacant or under-construction buildings, let your office know where you are."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.6 Reading a basic floor plan","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.6","text":"Before you design or install, you need to read the building. - Find the scale (for example 1/4\" = 1'). Use it to estimate wire runs. - Identify perimeter openings : exterior doors, windows, garage doors, basement access. - Identify interior traffic paths : hallways and rooms an intruder must pass through. That's where motion detectors go. - Find a panel location : usually a closet, basement, or utility room, near an outlet, out of sight from entry doors, and with a reasonable path for wiring. - Mark the entry/exit door the customer uses most. The keypad goes there. Common symbols you'll mark on a plan: C (contact), M or PIR (motion), GB (glass break), K (keypad), S (siren), P (panel), SD (smoke detector)."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 1: Foundations","where":"Learn","href":"learn/1","text":"Who it's for: brand-new techs, or anyone who wants a refresher on electrical basics. You'll be able to: use Ohm's law on the job, tell series from parallel circuits, explain what makes a circuit Class 2, pick the right tools, and stay safe on site."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.1 The parts of a system","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.1","text":"Part What it does Control panel The brain. A circuit board in a metal can with a transformer and battery. It watches every zone, decides what's an alarm, sounds the siren, and sends signals out. Keypad The user interface. Arms, disarms, shows status, and is where you program the system. Some systems use a touchscreen or phone app instead of or alongside it. Sensors (initiating devices) Detect something happening: a door opening, motion, breaking glass. Siren / sounder Makes noise inside (and sometimes outside) on alarm. Communicator Sends alarm signals to the central station: cellular, internet (IP), or phone line. Power Transformer (AC from the wall) plus a backup battery inside the panel. Expanders / modules Add zones, wireless receivers, outputs, or more power."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.2 Zones","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.2","text":"A zone is an input the panel watches. It might be one device (a motion detector) or several devices wired together (all the windows in a bedroom). Each zone has: - A number and a name (\"Zone 3, Kitchen Windows\") - A zone type that tells the panel how to react (entry/exit, perimeter, interior, 24-hour; see Module 7) - Attributes like chime, bypassable, or swinger shutdown Why it matters: the more zones you use, the more precise the central station's report is. \"Zone 7, Back Door\" helps police more than \"Zone 1, All Doors and Windows\". Many techs put each door on its own zone and group windows by room. Field tip (David to add): how you decide when to combine devices on one zone."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.3 Partitions","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.3","text":"A partition (also called an area) splits one panel into separate systems that arm and disarm independently. Examples: - A house with a detached garage or in-law apartment - A small business with a front office and a warehouse - A strip of two storefronts sharing one panel Each partition has its own zones, user codes, and keypads. A user can be given access to one partition or several."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.4 Arming modes","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.4","text":"Mode Common names What's armed Typical use Away Away, Full Perimeter and interior Nobody home Stay Stay, Home, Perimeter Perimeter only; interior motion is ignored People home, moving around Night Night, Night-Stay Perimeter plus selected interior zones Everyone in bed; downstairs motion armed Instant Instant, Max Like Stay or Away but no entry delay Extra-secure when nobody should come in"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.5 The signal path","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.5","text":"What happens when a burglar opens the back door while the system is armed Away: 1. The contact on the door opens. 2. The zone loop resistance changes, and the panel sees it. 3. The zone is an entry/exit type, so the panel starts the entry delay and the keypad beeps. 4. Nobody enters a code. The entry delay expires. 5. The panel goes into alarm : the siren sounds. 6. The panel holds the signal for a short abort window (if programmed) in case the user disarms late. 7. The communicator sends the alarm (account number, event code, zone number) to the central station over cellular or IP. 8. The central station receiver decodes it and puts it on an operator's screen. 9. The operator follows the account's action plan : typically call the premises and/or keyholders to verify (Enhanced Call Verification), then dispatch police if it isn't canceled. 10. The siren times out after the programmed bell cutoff time and the system resets, ready to alarm again."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.6 Supervision and troubles","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.6","text":"The panel doesn't only watch for alarms. It also watches itself and reports troubles : - AC power loss - Low or missing battery - Communication failure - Zone tamper or wire fault - Wireless sensor supervision loss or low battery - Keypad or module communication loss Troubles usually make the keypad beep and show a message, and many are reported to the central station. Clearing troubles is a big part of service work (see the Troubleshoot tab)."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 2: How an Intrusion System Works","where":"Learn","href":"learn/2","text":"You'll be able to: name every major part of an alarm system, explain zones and partitions, and trace an alarm from the sensor to the police dispatcher."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.1 Door and window contacts","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.1","text":"A contact is a reed switch plus a magnet . The switch mounts on the frame, the magnet on the moving door or window. When they're together, the switch is closed (on a normally closed contact). Separate them and it opens. Type Where it's used Notes Surface Doors and windows where you can't drill Easy to install, visible, can be knocked off Recessed (plunger-style bore) Wood doors and windows Hidden, durable; drill frame and door in line with each other Overhead door Garage and roll-up doors Heavy-duty, armored cable, wide gap; floor mount or track mount Wide-gap Loose-fitting doors, steel doors Stronger magnet allows a bigger gap Gap is the distance at which the switch opens. Typical surface contacts operate around 3/4\" to 1\" gap; overhead door contacts are rated for 1.5\" to 2\" or more. Steel doors and frames reduce gap , sometimes by half; use contacts rated for steel. Common mistakes - Magnet and switch misaligned, so the zone flickers when the door rattles in wind - Recessed holes not lined up, so the door has to slam to close the zone - Using a standard contact on a steel door"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.2 Motion detectors (PIR)","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.2","text":"A passive infrared (PIR) detector doesn't send anything out. It watches for a change in infrared heat across its zones. A body-temperature object moving from one detection zone to the next triggers it. Key point: PIRs are most sensitive to motion across the pattern, and least sensitive to someone walking straight toward the detector. Aim them so an intruder would cross the coverage. Mounting - Most wall-mounted PIRs are designed for a specific height, commonly about 7 to 7.5 feet. Mounting at the wrong height shrinks coverage or creates dead zones. - Corner mounting gives the widest coverage of a room. - Ceiling-mount 360° PIRs are good for open areas and rooms with lots of obstructions. Avoid aiming at (these cause false alarms) - Heat sources: furnaces, space heaters, fireplaces, wood stoves - Air vents blowing directly on the detector - Windows getting direct sunlight - Moving objects: ceiling fans, curtains near vents, balloons - Stairs that a large dog can climb (even with pet-immune models) Pet immunity lets animals up to a rated weight (often 40 to 80 lb) move without triggering, but only when mounted exactly as the manufacturer specifies, and only if pets can't climb above the lower detection zone."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.3 Dual-technology motion","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.3","text":"A dual-tech detector combines PIR with microwave (which bounces radio waves and senses motion by Doppler shift). Both technologies must trip at the same time to alarm. - Much more resistant to false alarms in tough environments (garages, warehouses, rooms with heat sources) - Microwave goes through glass and thin walls, so aim it carefully and adjust its range so it doesn't see outside - Draws more current than a plain PIR, so account for it in your battery calculation"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.4 Glass break detectors","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.4","text":"An acoustic glass break detector listens for the sound signature of glass breaking: typically a low-frequency \"flex\" (the thud of the impact) followed by a high-frequency shatter. - Covers several windows in a room at once, often up to about 20 to 25 feet - Mount on a wall or ceiling with a clear line of sight to the glass - Heavy drapes or shutters block the sound; plan for them - Test with a glass break simulator made for that detector, not by clapping or banging - Doesn't detect a window that's opened or a pane removed quietly; pair it with contacts or motion Shock (vibration) sensors mount on the glass or frame and detect the impact itself. Useful on single panes or where a room is noisy."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.5 Smoke and heat on a burglar panel","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.5","text":"Many residential panels also accept smoke and heat detectors. Key differences from intrusion devices: - Use devices listed for that specific panel (4-wire smoke detectors need a resettable power output; 2-wire smokes need a compatible zone) - Fire zones are 24-hour and are always active, armed or not - Fire signals get priority at the central station - Residential fire installed on a burglar panel still has code requirements (NFPA 72 chapter on household signaling) Full fire alarm coverage comes in the Fire pack later. For now: if it's fire, follow the panel manual exactly and check with your supervisor."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.6 Panic, hold-up, and environmental","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.6","text":"Device What it does Zone type Panic button User presses for help; audible or silent 24-hour audible or silent panic Hold-up / duress button Silent robbery alarm at a business counter 24-hour silent hold-up Medical pendant Wireless button for medical emergency 24-hour medical Water / flood sensor Detects water on the floor 24-hour supervisory or auxiliary Low temperature Freeze warning in vacation homes 24-hour supervisory CO detector Carbon monoxide 24-hour CO (use listed devices) Hold-up buttons must be placed where staff can reach them without being noticed, and the signal must be silent. Make sure the customer knows the difference between panic and hold-up."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 3: Sensors and Devices","where":"Learn","href":"learn/3","text":"You'll be able to: choose the right sensor for each opening or area, explain how each one detects, and avoid the placement mistakes that cause false alarms."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.1 Why end-of-line resistors exist","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.1","text":"Without a resistor, a panel can only tell \"closed\" from \"open\". A cut wire looks exactly like an open door, and a burglar who shorts the wires together makes the zone look normal forever. An end-of-line (EOL) resistor placed at the last device on the loop gives the panel a known resistance to watch for. Now the panel can tell three states apart: What the panel reads Meaning About the EOL value Normal Open (infinite resistance) Alarm or trouble, depending on wiring style Short (near 0 Ω) Alarm or trouble, depending on wiring style The resistor goes at the end of the run, at the last device, not at the panel. A resistor installed at the panel terminals supervises nothing; the panel can't see a cut or short in the wire. Some inspectors and companies require it at the device for this reason. EOL values depend on the panel brand. Common values include 1 kΩ, 2 kΩ, 2.2 kΩ, 4.7 kΩ, 5.6 kΩ, and 10 kΩ. Always use the value in the panel's manual."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.2 Zone wiring styles","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.2","text":"Normally closed (NC), single EOL Contacts wired in series , resistor in series at the last device. - Normal: EOL value - Any device opens or wire cut: open → alarm - Wires shorted together: near 0 Ω → panel sees trouble or alarm (depends on panel) This is the most common style for doors and windows. Normally open (NO), single EOL Devices wired in parallel , resistor across the loop at the last device. - Normal: EOL value - Any device closes: short → alarm - Wire cut: open → trouble Used for some panic buttons, older glass breaks, and some environmental sensors. Double end-of-line (DEOL) Each device gets two resistors : one in series with the loop and one across (in parallel with) the contact. Many panels use two resistors of the same value. With two equal resistors of value R: - Contact closed (normal): the parallel resistor is shorted out, so the panel reads R - Contact open (alarm): both resistors are in series, so the panel reads 2R - Wire cut or tamper opened: open → tamper/trouble - Wires shorted: near 0 Ω → fault DEOL lets the panel tell an alarm from a tamper or wire fault on the same zone. Check the manual for the exact resistor values and arrangement. No EOL (closed loop) Some panels let you disable EOL supervision. Normal = short, alarm = open. It's quick but unsupervised; avoid it unless the panel or job requires it. Field tip (David to add): the wiring style you see most often in the field, and how to spot it when you take over another company's system."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.3 Reading a zone with a meter","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.3","text":"1. Disconnect the zone wires from the panel terminals (or test at the device end). 2. Set the meter to ohms. 3. Measure across the two zone wires. You read Single EOL NC loop means EOL value (within about 10%) Zone is good and closed OL / open A device is open, a splice failed, or a wire is broken Near 0 Ω Short somewhere (staple through the cable, pinched wire, wrong wiring) A different value Wrong resistor, two resistors, or corrosion/bad splice adding resistance Also check for ground faults: measure from each zone wire to earth ground. You should read open. Any reading means a wire is touching something grounded."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.4 Wire types and gauges","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.4","text":"AWG (American Wire Gauge): a smaller number is a thicker wire. Typical use Common cable Door/window contacts, zones 22/2 (22 AWG, 2 conductors) Motion detectors (zone + power) 22/4 Keypads and data bus 22/4 (some panels allow or require 18/4 on long runs) Sirens and bells 18/2 Power supply runs, long device power runs 18/2 or 18/4, heavier as needed Transformer to panel 18/2 or 16/2 per manual - Use stranded wire where it flexes and solid where your terminals and practice call for it; follow the manufacturer. - Use the correct jacket rating for the space (CL2, CL2R riser, CL2P plenum, outdoor/direct-burial rated outside). - Leave a service loop at the panel and devices. Use the Wire Gauge and Voltage Drop calculators for long runs."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.5 Home runs vs data bus","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.5","text":"Home run (hardwired zones): each zone has its own cable back to the panel or a zone expander. Data bus (keypad bus, addressable): keypads, zone expanders, wireless receivers, and output modules share one 4-wire bus: two for power, two for data. Each device needs a unique address . Bus rules to remember (exact limits vary by panel; check the manual): - Total bus wire length and per-device length limits - Star (home-run) vs daisy-chain topology allowed - Don't run the bus next to AC power or lighting ballasts - Wire colors vary by manufacturer; match the terminal labels, not the color you're used to"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.6 Powering devices","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.6","text":"The panel has an auxiliary (aux) power output (typically around 12 VDC) with a maximum current rating. Every powered device (motion detectors, keypads, glass breaks, modules) adds to that load. Rules - Add up the standby current of everything on aux. It must stay under the panel's aux rating. - Add up the alarm current too (sirens and bell output have their own rating). - Your battery must carry the standby load for the required hours plus the alarm load for the required minutes (use the Battery Standby calculator). - If you exceed the panel's rating, add a supervised auxiliary power supply with its own transformer and battery. - Fused or PTC-protected outputs trip on overload; a dead keypad or motion bank can be a tripped aux output."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.7 Grounding, wire management, and labeling","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.7","text":"- Earth ground: connect the panel's earth-ground terminal to a proper ground (often a cold water pipe or ground rod per manufacturer and local code). It protects the panel from lightning and static. - Separation: keep low-voltage cable away from 120 V runs, fluorescent fixtures, and motors. Cross power wiring at right angles. - Support: use cable staples sized for the cable and don't pinch it. A staple through the jacket causes intermittent shorts that are hard to find. - Splices: solder or use gel-filled connectors. Avoid twisted-and-taped splices. - Labeling: label every cable at both ends with the zone number or device. Leave a zone list and wiring chart in the panel can."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 4: Wiring and Circuits","where":"Learn","href":"learn/4","text":"You'll be able to: wire zones in every common style, read a zone with a meter, size wire, power devices correctly, and leave a clean, labeled install."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.1 How wireless works","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.1","text":"A wireless sensor is a normal sensor (contact, PIR, smoke) with a small radio transmitter and a battery. When it changes state, it sends a coded message to a receiver that's built into the panel or connected on the bus. Each sensor has a unique serial number (also called the TXID or ESN). You enroll that number into a zone so the panel knows which sensor is which. Two broad kinds: - One-way: the sensor transmits; the panel can't talk back. Simpler and cheaper. - Two-way: the panel confirms each message and can send commands back (for example, change sensitivity). More reliable, better battery management. Wireless sensors from one manufacturer generally only work with that manufacturer's receivers, and different series from the same brand may not mix. Confirm compatibility before ordering."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.2 Supervision","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.2","text":"Because a wireless sensor isn't wired, the panel can't measure a loop. Instead, each sensor sends a periodic check-in (supervision) signal. If the panel doesn't hear a sensor within its supervision window , it shows a supervision loss (sometimes \"missing\" or \"check\") trouble for that zone. - Supervision windows vary by panel and listing, commonly a few hours up to 24 hours - Some devices (like handheld remotes and panic pendants) are usually enrolled unsupervised because they leave the building - Fire and life-safety wireless devices must be supervised"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.3 Enrolling sensors","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.3","text":"General steps (exact keystrokes vary by panel): 1. Enter installer programming. 2. Choose the zone number and set its zone type and attributes. 3. Enroll the sensor's serial: either type it in, or put the panel in learn mode and trip the sensor (or pull its tamper) to transmit. 4. Set which loop/input of the sensor to use if the device has more than one. 5. Exit programming and test the zone. Record every serial number and location on the zone list. Field tip (David to add): your process for keeping serials straight when enrolling 20+ sensors."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.4 Range and site survey","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.4","text":"Wireless range on the spec sheet is measured in open air. Real buildings cut it down. Things that kill signal - Metal: steel doors and frames, metal siding, foil-backed insulation, mirrors, appliances, ductwork - Concrete and brick, especially with rebar - Water: aquariums, water heaters, people in the way - Distance and multiple walls Before you mount - Hold the sensor where it'll go and trip it while watching the panel's signal strength reading (most panels show this in a test mode). - Aim for the \"good\" or better range the manufacturer recommends; marginal signal will become a supervision trouble later. - Move the receiver to a central spot if many sensors are weak, or add a repeater if the system supports one. - Don't mount the panel or receiver inside a metal enclosure or right next to the electrical panel. RF jamming: many panels detect a jammer and can report it. If you get jam troubles, look for other wireless equipment on nearby frequencies."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.5 Batteries","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.5","text":"- Most sensors use lithium batteries rated for roughly 3 to 5 years, but heavy-traffic doors and frequently triggered motions drain faster. - The panel shows a low battery trouble for the sensor before it dies, usually with weeks of warning. - Use the exact battery type the manufacturer lists. A wrong battery may fit but have the wrong voltage or chemistry. - Note battery replacement dates on the service record. Some companies replace all sensor batteries on a schedule."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.6 Hybrid systems","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.6","text":"A hybrid system combines a hardwired panel with a wireless receiver. This is common when: - Existing wired zones work fine, but new openings are hard to wire - Adding sensors to a finished home - Upgrading an older wired system without rewiring Plan zone numbers so wired and wireless zones are clearly separated on the zone list."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 5: Wireless Systems","where":"Learn","href":"learn/5","text":"You'll be able to: explain how wireless sensors talk to a panel, enroll and test them, plan for range, and manage battery life."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.1 Site survey","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.1","text":"Walk the whole property with the customer before you design anything. Ask the customer - Who lives or works here? Pets (weight, do they climb)? Kids? Elderly? - Which door do you use most? - What are you most worried about? (Break-in while away, while sleeping, a specific valuables room, a detached garage) - Any previous break-ins or false alarm history? - Do you want smoke, CO, water, or cameras later? Check the building - Every exterior door, window, and basement or crawlspace access - Construction: drywall, plaster, block, finished vs unfinished basement and attic (this decides wired vs wireless) - Panel location options near an outlet - Cell signal strength at the panel location - Existing system or wiring you can reuse - Local alarm permit requirements (see Module 8)"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.2 Designing the system","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.2","text":"Layers of protection 1. Perimeter: contacts on exterior doors and accessible windows; glass break where glass can be broken. 2. Interior: motion detectors covering traffic paths (main hallway, stairs, rooms with valuables). 3. Life safety: smoke, heat, CO if part of the job. 4. Annunciation and communication: keypads, sirens, communicator. Design rules of thumb - Every exterior door gets a contact. - Ground-floor and accessible windows get contacts or glass break coverage. - Place at least one motion so an intruder can't get from any ground-floor opening to the bedrooms or valuables without crossing it. - Primary keypad within reach of the main entry door; second keypad by the master bedroom or garage entry as needed. - Group devices into zones so the central station report tells police where to go. Write a zone list before you start: zone number, name, device type, zone type, wired or wireless."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.3 Device placement quick rules","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.3","text":"Device Do Don't Door contact Mount on the latch side at the top of the door Mount on the hinge side (smallest gap movement) Window contact Mount on the moving sash Leave windows that open with no contact PIR Mount at the rated height, aimed across traffic Aim at windows, heat sources, vents, fans Glass break Line of sight to the glass, within rated range Mount on the same wall as the glass if the manufacturer prohibits it, or behind heavy drapes Keypad Inside, near entry, about 4 to 5 ft high Visible through a window from outside Interior siren Central location, out of easy reach Right next to the keypad where it's easily silenced Exterior siren/strobe High, hard to reach, visible from the street Within easy reach"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.4 Mounting the panel and power","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.4","text":"1. Mount the can on a solid surface at a comfortable working height, with room around it for wiring. 2. Bring all cables in neatly through knockouts. Use grommets or bushings. 3. Connect earth ground per the manual. 4. Run the transformer wire. Use the transformer the manual specifies, plugged into a non-switched outlet. Secure it with its retaining screw if it has one. 5. Land all zones, bus devices, and outputs before applying power. 6. Power-up order: connect the battery first or AC first as the manual says. Many manuals say AC first, then battery. Follow your panel's instructions. 7. Verify voltage at aux and bell outputs under load."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.5 Keypads and sirens","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.5","text":"- Address each keypad before or after landing it, as the manual says. Duplicate addresses cause bus trouble. - Confirm keypad backlight and display at the farthest keypad; low voltage there means a voltage drop problem. - Siren wiring is polarity-sensitive on most sirens: positive to bell +, negative to bell −. - Some panels supervise the bell circuit and need an EOL resistor on it."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.6 Communicators","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.6","text":"Path Pros Cons Cellular Works when phone/internet lines are cut; most common today Needs a usable signal; network sunsets require upgrades IP (internet) Fast, low cost Fails if the router or internet is down or the customer changes equipment Dual-path (cell + IP) Redundant; often required for commercial More cost POTS phone line Legacy Copper landlines are being retired in many areas Install steps - Check signal strength at the panel location before you mount. Use the communicator's own signal readout. - If signal is weak, use a remote antenna or relocate the communicator (within the manufacturer's limits). - Activate the communicator on the provider's portal and link it to the central station account. - Send test signals and confirm with the central station that they arrived with the right account and zone. Field tip (David to add): what you do when the only weak-signal spot is where the customer wants the panel."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 6: Installation","where":"Learn","href":"learn/6","text":"You'll be able to: survey a site, design a system, place every device correctly, and install the panel, keypads, sirens, and communicator."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.1 Zone types","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.1","text":"Names vary by brand; the behavior is what matters. Zone type Behavior Typical devices Entry/Exit (delay) Starts the entry delay when tripped while armed; ignored during exit delay Main entry door, garage entry door Perimeter (instant) Alarms immediately when armed in Stay or Away Other exterior doors, windows, glass break Interior follower Instant, unless an entry/exit zone was tripped first, then it follows the entry delay. Bypassed in Stay Motion and interior doors on the entry path Interior instant Instant in Away; bypassed in Stay Motion away from the entry path 24-hour audible / silent Always active, armed or not Panic, hold-up 24-hour fire Always active, fire priority Smoke, heat 24-hour supervisory / auxiliary Always active, non-burglary Water, low temperature Day zone / trouble by day Trouble beep when disarmed, alarm when armed Emergency exit doors at a business Why \"interior follower\" matters: when the homeowner comes in through the front door and walks past the hallway motion, the motion must give them the entry delay. If you program that motion as interior instant, it alarms before they reach the keypad."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.2 Entry and exit delays","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.2","text":"- Exit delay: time after arming to leave. Entry delay: time after opening an entry door to disarm. - The ANSI/SIA CP-01 false alarm standard sets minimums and defaults for these: for example, a minimum exit delay of 45 seconds (default 60) and entry delay minimum of 30 seconds, with a combined entry delay plus abort window not to exceed 1 minute. - Set delays long enough for the customer's real path (garage door to keypad with groceries), but not so long that an intruder gets far. - Multiple entry delays are available on many panels (short for the front door, longer for the garage)."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.3 User codes and authority levels","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.3","text":"- Installer code: your access to programming. Change it from the factory default on every install; leaving defaults is a security hole. - Master code: the customer's top code; can add and delete users. - User codes: for family members or employees; can be limited by partition, schedule, or function. - Duress code: disarms normally but silently sends a duress signal. - Temporary / one-time codes: for cleaners, contractors. Teach the customer to give each person their own code so event history shows who armed and disarmed."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.4 Central station setup","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.4","text":"You need from the central station or your dealer portal: - Account number for the site - Receiver phone number or IP/cellular path (often pre-set by the communicator provider) - Reporting format: most panels today use Contact ID or SIA format Then program: - Which events report (alarms, restores, troubles, openings/closings, tests) - Periodic test timer (commonly every 24 hours, sometimes more often for commercial) so the central station knows the communicator is alive - Abort window (also called communication delay) and cancel reporting per CP-01 - Zone list sent to the central station with names, zone types, and the customer's call list"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.5 Testing and commissioning","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.5","text":"1. Put the account on test with the central station first. Never trip a live system without doing this. 2. Walk test every zone. Most panels have a walk-test mode that chimes each zone as it trips. - Open every door and window with a contact. - Walk every motion pattern from all likely approaches. - Use the simulator on every glass break. - Pull every tamper you can. 3. Test communication. Arm the system, trip a zone, let it alarm, and confirm with the central station that they received the alarm with the right zone number and name. Test troubles too (AC loss, low battery if practical). 4. Test power. Unplug AC and confirm the system runs on battery and reports AC loss. Check battery voltage under load. 5. Take the account off test , confirming with the operator. 6. Document: zone list, device locations, serial numbers, test results, battery install date, communicator ID."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.6 Customer training and handoff","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.6","text":"A trained customer is the best way to prevent false alarms. Cover, with the customer doing it themselves: - Arming Away, Stay, and Night, and what each does - Disarming, and what to do if they set it off by mistake (disarm, then answer the central station's call and give their passcode) - Their verbal passcode for the central station - Bypassing a zone, and why not to leave windows bypassed forever - What troubles look like and who to call - How to use the phone app if there is one - Panic buttons and what they do - Keeping the call list up to date Leave a printed or digital quick-start sheet and the zone list. Field tip (David to add): the one thing customers always forget after handoff."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 7: Programming and Commissioning","where":"Learn","href":"learn/7","text":"You'll be able to: program zone types, delays, user codes, and central station reporting, then fully test the system and hand it off to the customer."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.1 The AHJ","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.1","text":"The Authority Having Jurisdiction (AHJ) is the office or person who enforces the rules where you're working: often a city or county building department, the fire marshal, or the police department's alarm unit. The AHJ decides: - Whether a permit is required for the install - Which code editions apply - Whether an inspection is required - Alarm registration and false alarm fines When in doubt, call the AHJ before the job."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.2 Licensing, permits, and alarm ordinances","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.2","text":"- Many states and cities require alarm company and/or technician licenses , sometimes with background checks and continuing education. Know what your state requires before you work. - Many cities require the customer to register the alarm (alarm permit) before police will respond. - Alarm ordinances often set false alarm fees, bell cutoff time limits for exterior sirens, and verification requirements (such as Enhanced Call Verification or video/audio verified response). - Some jurisdictions use verified response : police won't respond to a burglar alarm unless it's verified by video, audio, or a person on site."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.3 Key standards","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.3","text":"Standard What it covers ANSI/SIA CP-01 Control panel features for false alarm reduction (delays, abort window, swinger shutdown, exit error, and more) NFPA 70 (National Electrical Code) Wiring rules, including power-limited (Class 2) circuits and cable ratings NFPA 731 Installation of electronic premises security systems NFPA 72 Fire alarm and household fire warning equipment (applies when you install smoke/heat/CO on a panel) UL 681 Installation and classification of burglar alarm systems UL 1023 Household burglar alarm system units UL 1610 / UL 1076 Central station and proprietary burglar alarm units UL 827 Central station alarm services UL certificated systems: some commercial customers (often due to insurance) need a UL certificated burglar alarm system. That means a listed alarm company, listed equipment, installation to UL 681, and a certificate issued for the specific system. This goes beyond v1 but you should know it exists and when to call your supervisor."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.4 False alarm reduction","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.4","text":"Most alarm dispatches are false alarms, and most false alarms are user error , followed by equipment and installation problems. Every one costs the customer fines and police time. CP-01 panel features (typical; check the panel and standard for exact defaults): Feature What it does Exit delay progress / annunciation Keypad beeps during exit delay, faster near the end Exit error If an entry door opens and closes during exit delay but isn't closed when the delay ends, the panel goes into entry delay and sends an exit error code instead of an instant alarm Auto Stay If armed Away but nobody leaves through an entry door, the panel automatically arms Stay Abort window Holds the alarm signal briefly so a quick disarm cancels it Cancel report If disarmed after the signal was sent, sends a cancel message Swinger shutdown Limits how many times one zone can trip in an armed period Cross-zoning Requires two zones to trip within a time window before alarming (use with care) Recent closing Reports when an alarm happens shortly after arming Duress Silent signal with a special code Installer practices that prevent false alarms - Correct PIR placement and pet settings (Module 3) - Tight contact alignment and correct gaps - Interior follower zones on the entry path (Module 7) - Batteries replaced on schedule - Thorough customer training (Module 7)"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.5 Documentation","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.5","text":"Leave and keep: - Zone list with device type, location, zone type, wired/wireless, serial numbers - Wiring diagram or panel drawing for anything non-obvious - Programming summary or a saved programming file (stored securely) - Test results and central station confirmation - Battery install dates - Permit number and inspection results, if any - Signed customer acceptance and training acknowledgment Good documentation saves the next service call, and protects you and your company if something goes wrong."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 8: Codes, Standards, and False Alarm Reduction","where":"Learn","href":"learn/8","text":"You'll be able to: explain who sets the rules for an alarm install, name the key standards, use false alarm reduction features, and document a job properly. These lessons give an overview. Codes are adopted and amended locally, and editions change. Always confirm requirements with your company, the AHJ, and the current adopted edition."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Zone wiring at a glance","where":"Reference","href":"reference/zone-wiring-at-a-glance","text":""},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Meter readings on a zone","where":"Reference","href":"reference/meter-readings-on-a-zone","text":"Reading (wires off the panel) Single EOL NC Single EOL NO DEOL (equal R) ≈ EOL value Normal Normal Normal (contact closed) ≈ 2 × EOL Two resistors in series (miswire) Miswire Alarm (contact open) ≈ ½ × EOL Two resistors in parallel (miswire) Two EOLs installed Miswire OL / open Device open or wire cut Wire cut Wire cut / tamper ≈ 0 Ω Short Device closed or short Short Any reading to earth ground Ground fault Ground fault Ground fault Tolerance: within about ±10% of the expected value is normal. Higher than expected, with no open, usually means a corroded or loose splice."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Common EOL resistor values","where":"Reference","href":"reference/common-eol-resistor-values","text":"Always use the value the panel manual calls for. Common values seen in the field: Value Color bands (4-band, 5% gold) 1 kΩ Brown, Black, Red, Gold 2 kΩ Red, Black, Red, Gold 2.2 kΩ Red, Red, Red, Gold 4.7 kΩ Yellow, Violet, Red, Gold 5.6 kΩ Green, Blue, Red, Gold 10 kΩ Brown, Black, Orange, Gold Color code: Black 0, Brown 1, Red 2, Orange 3, Yellow 4, Green 5, Blue 6, Violet 7, Gray 8, White 9. Bands 1 and 2 are digits, band 3 is the number of zeros, band 4 is tolerance."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Copper wire resistance","where":"Reference","href":"reference/copper-wire-resistance","text":"Approximate resistance of solid copper at 68 °F (20 °C), per conductor: AWG Ω per 1,000 ft Ω per foot 22 16.1 0.0161 20 10.2 0.0102 18 6.39 0.00639 16 4.02 0.00402 14 2.53 0.00253 12 1.59 0.00159 Voltage drop = 2 × one-way length (ft) × current (A) × Ω per foot. The 2 accounts for the wire out and back. Use the Voltage Drop calculator."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Battery standby formula","where":"Reference","href":"reference/battery-standby-formula","text":"Required Ah = [(total standby current in A × standby hours) + (total alarm current in A × alarm hours)] × 1.2 safety factor - Alarm minutes ÷ 60 = alarm hours (for example 4 min = 0.067 h) - Round up to the next standard battery size (common sizes 4, 5, 7, 8, 12, 18 Ah) - Commonly cited standby requirements: about 4 hours for UL residential burglary, 24 hours for UL commercial/certificated burglary, 24 hours for fire - Check the panel's maximum battery size and charger capability Use the Battery Standby calculator."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Zone type cheat sheet","where":"Reference","href":"reference/zone-type-cheat-sheet","text":"Type Armed Away Armed Stay Disarmed Entry/Exit Delay Delay Chime (if set) Perimeter Instant Instant Chime (if set) Interior follower Instant, or delay after entry zone Bypassed Ignored Interior instant Instant Bypassed Ignored 24-hour panic/hold-up Alarm Alarm Alarm 24-hour fire Fire alarm Fire alarm Fire alarm 24-hour supervisory Report Report Report Day zone Alarm Alarm Trouble beep"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Device placement quick rules","where":"Reference","href":"reference/device-placement-quick-rules","text":"- Door contact: latch side, top of door. Steel doors cut gap; use steel-rated contacts. - Overhead door contact: floor mount at the leading edge or track mount; armored cable. - PIR: rated height (commonly 7 to 7.5 ft), aimed across traffic. Not at heat, vents, sunlit windows, fans. - Pet-immune PIR: exact rated height and orientation; no furniture or stairs pets can climb in view. - Dual-tech: turn microwave range down so it doesn't see through walls or windows. - Glass break: clear line of sight, within rated range (commonly up to about 20 to 25 ft); test with the listed simulator. - Keypad: inside near entry, not visible from outside. - Siren: central, out of reach. Exterior: high and visible. - Wireless: check signal strength before mounting; avoid metal."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Contact ID event codes (common)","where":"Reference","href":"reference/contact-id-event-codes-common","text":"Contact ID message: ACCT 18 Q EEE GG ZZZ - ACCT = 4-digit account, 18 = message type, Q = qualifier (1 = new event/opening, 3 = restore/closing), EEE = event code, GG = partition, ZZZ = zone or user number Code Event Code Event 100 Medical 301 AC loss 110 Fire 302 Low system battery 120 Panic 344 RF receiver jam 121 Duress 350 Communication trouble 122 Silent panic 373 Fire trouble 130 Burglary 380 Sensor trouble 131 Perimeter burglary 381 Loss of RF supervision 132 Interior burglary 383 Sensor tamper 134 Entry/exit burglary 384 RF low battery 137 Tamper 401 Open/close by user 139 Verified intrusion 406 Cancel 145 Expansion module tamper 570 Zone bypass 150 24-hour non-burglary 602 Periodic test"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Communicator quick facts","where":"Reference","href":"reference/communicator-quick-facts","text":"- Check cell signal at the panel before mounting; use the communicator's own signal readout. - Weak signal: remote antenna, relocate within manufacturer limits, or dual-path. - After install: activate on the provider portal, link to central station account, send test signals, confirm account and zone at the station. - Periodic test commonly every 24 h; more often for commercial or certificated systems."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Before you leave the job","where":"Reference","href":"reference/before-you-leave-the-job","text":"- [ ] Account taken off test, confirmed with the operator - [ ] All zones walk-tested and reported correctly - [ ] AC loss and battery verified - [ ] Installer code changed from default - [ ] Customer trained and knows their passcode - [ ] Zone list and documentation left in panel and uploaded - [ ] Panel can closed and tamper restored - [ ] Work area cleaned"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Glossary","where":"Reference","href":"reference/glossary","text":"- AHJ: Authority Having Jurisdiction; the office that enforces codes where you work. - Abort window: delay before an alarm is reported so a quick disarm cancels it. - Aux power: the panel's DC output for powering devices. - Bus: shared 4-wire power and data connection for keypads and modules. - Bypass: temporarily ignore a zone. - Central station: monitoring center that receives signals and dispatches. - Contact ID / SIA: common formats for reporting events to the central station. - CP-01: ANSI/SIA standard for false alarm reduction features in control panels. - DEOL: double end-of-line; two resistors per zone to tell alarm from tamper. - ECV: Enhanced Call Verification; two calls to verify before dispatch. - EOL: end-of-line resistor; lets the panel supervise the zone wiring. - NC / NO: normally closed / normally open. - Partition: a group of zones armed and disarmed as its own system. - PIR: passive infrared motion detector. - Supervision: the panel checking that devices and paths are working. - Swinger shutdown: limit on repeated alarms from one zone. - Tamper: a switch or circuit that detects a device or cover being opened. - Zone: one input the panel monitors."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"AHJ","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"Authority Having Jurisdiction; the office that enforces codes where you work."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Abort window","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"delay before an alarm is reported so a quick disarm cancels it."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Aux power","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the panel's DC output for powering devices."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Bus","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"shared 4-wire power and data connection for keypads and modules."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Bypass","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"temporarily ignore a zone."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Central station","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"monitoring center that receives signals and dispatches."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Contact ID / SIA","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"common formats for reporting events to the central station."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"CP-01","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"ANSI/SIA standard for false alarm reduction features in control panels."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"DEOL","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"double end-of-line; two resistors per zone to tell alarm from tamper."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"ECV","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"Enhanced Call Verification; two calls to verify before dispatch."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"EOL","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"end-of-line resistor; lets the panel supervise the zone wiring."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"NC / NO","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"normally closed / normally open."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Partition","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a group of zones armed and disarmed as its own system."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"PIR","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"passive infrared motion detector."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Supervision","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the panel checking that devices and paths are working."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Swinger shutdown","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"limit on repeated alarms from one zone."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Tamper","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"a switch or circuit that detects a device or cover being opened."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Zone","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"one input the panel monitors."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Zone shows trouble or fault","where":"Guide 1","href":"troubleshoot/1","text":"Symptom: keypad shows a trouble, fault, or \"check\" on a hardwired zone. 1. Note the zone number and look it up on the zone list. Is it a wireless zone? → Go to Guide 7. 2. Look at the device. Is it physically damaged, missing, or its cover off? → Repair or replace; restore the tamper. 3. Remove the zone wires from the panel and meter them (Reference: Meter readings on a zone ). - Reads normal EOL value → wiring is good. Check the panel terminal (loose screw, corrosion) and the zone's programmed EOL setting. Swap the wires to a known good zone to see if the problem follows the wire or stays with the panel input. - Reads 0 Ω (short) → go to step 4. - Reads open → go to step 5. - Reads a wrong value → wrong or extra resistor, or a high-resistance splice. Find the EOL and check every splice. 4. Short: look for a staple through the cable, a pinched wire behind trim, a crushed cable in a door frame, or water in a splice. Split the run in half at an accessible device and test each half to narrow it down. 5. Open: check each contact with its magnet in place (it should read closed). Check splices and the EOL itself. Split the run at accessible points to narrow it down. 6. After repair, meter again, re-land, confirm the zone restores, and walk-test it. Field tip (David to add): fastest way you've found to locate a short in a finished wall."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Zone won't restore / system won't arm","where":"Guide 2","href":"troubleshoot/2","text":"Symptom: keypad shows \"not ready\" or a zone open, and the customer can't arm. 1. Check the keypad for which zone is open. Is that door or window actually open? → Close it. 2. Close the opening. Does the zone still show open? - Check magnet alignment and gap. Has the door sagged or the window shifted? Realign or use a wide-gap contact. - Check for a missing magnet (painted over, knocked off, removed during window replacement). 3. If it's a motion, is something moving in its view (fan, curtains, pet, balloon)? The zone may restore after a few seconds of no motion. 4. Still open: meter the zone (Guide 1, step 3). 5. Short-term fix for the customer: bypass the zone so they can arm, and tell them clearly that the zone is unprotected until it's repaired. Note it on the ticket."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"False alarms from a specific zone","where":"Guide 3","href":"troubleshoot/3","text":"Symptom: the central station or customer reports repeated alarms from one zone with no break-in. 1. Pull the event history: what times, what arming mode, and was it the same zone each time? - Alarms right after arming or right at entry → likely user error or delay settings. Check entry/exit times, check that motions on the entry path are interior follower (Module 7), retrain the customer. 2. Contact: check gap and alignment. Does the zone open when you push or rattle the door? Wind and loose doors cause this. Tighten, realign, or replace with a wide-gap contact. 3. Motion: look for heat sources, HVAC vents, sunlight, curtains, fans, new furniture pets can climb, or a recent change in pets. Check mounting height and pet setting. Consider dual-tech or relocating. 4. Glass break: check sensitivity, nearby noise sources (dishes, dog barking, TV), and whether it was moved closer to a noise source. 5. Wiring: an intermittent open causes alarms with nothing visible. Meter the zone while wiggling the cable at the device, splices, and panel. Look for corroded splices in damp areas. 6. Wireless: check signal strength and battery. Weak signal can cause missed restores. See Guide 7. 7. Set or verify swinger shutdown so one bad zone can't keep dispatching while you fix it. 8. Document what you found and changed."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Low battery or AC loss","where":"Guide 4","href":"troubleshoot/4","text":"Symptom: keypad shows low battery, battery trouble, AC loss, or power trouble. 1. AC loss first: is the transformer plugged in? Is the outlet live (on a switch, tripped GFCI, or tripped breaker)? Reconnect or have the customer reset the breaker or GFCI. 2. Meter AC at the panel's AC terminals (AC volts). Expect about the transformer's rated output. Low or zero with the transformer plugged in → check the wire run, then the transformer. 3. Battery: disconnect the battery and meter it. - A 12 V sealed lead-acid battery at roughly 12.6 V or more is charged; much lower than 12 V usually means it's weak or dead. A battery reading OK unloaded can still fail under load. - Check the battery date. Many companies replace panel batteries every 3 to 5 years. 4. Check charging voltage at the panel's battery leads with the battery disconnected (commonly around 13.5 to 13.8 VDC). Low charge voltage points to a panel or transformer problem. 5. Replace the battery if it's old or fails a load test. Give it time to charge; many panels run a battery test periodically and may show the trouble until the next test or a manual test clears it. 6. Recurring low battery with a new battery: the standby load may be too high. Measure total aux current and run the Battery Standby calculator. Add a power supply if needed."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Keypad blank, dead, or \"system busy\"","where":"Guide 5","href":"troubleshoot/5","text":"Symptom: keypad display is blank, frozen, or shows busy/not available. 1. Are all keypads affected, or just one? - All keypads blank → panel power problem. Check AC and battery (Guide 4). Check the aux output fuse or PTC; a short on aux can shut down every keypad. Disconnect aux devices one group at a time to find the short. - One keypad → continue. 2. Meter voltage at the keypad's power terminals. Expect near panel aux voltage. - Low voltage → voltage drop (long run or thin wire) or a bad splice. Use the Voltage Drop calculator; upgrade wire or add a power supply. - Zero → open in the power wires. Check splices and terminals. 3. Check the keypad's address. A duplicate or out-of-range address causes errors or no display. 4. \"Busy\" or \"system not available\" can also mean another keypad or software is in programming mode, or the panel is still powering up. Wait a minute and exit programming at the other keypad or software. 5. Swap in a known good keypad at that location to tell keypad failure from wiring."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Bus or keypad communication errors","where":"Guide 6","href":"troubleshoot/6","text":"Symptom: module or keypad communication failure, bus fault, or supervision loss on a bus device. 1. Check the keypad or event history for which address is failing. 2. Check that the device's address is set correctly and isn't duplicated. 3. Meter power at the device under load. 4. Check the data wires: correct terminals (wire colors vary by brand), tight connections, no reversed data lines. 5. Check bus length and topology against the manual. Long runs or a mix of star and daisy chain can exceed limits. 6. Check for interference: bus cable running alongside AC power, fluorescent ballasts, or motors. 7. Disconnect bus devices one at a time (power down first, if the manual says so) to find a device that's dragging the bus down. 8. After a fix, many panels need the bus devices re-enrolled or supervision reset."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Wireless sensor supervision loss","where":"Guide 7","href":"troubleshoot/7","text":"Symptom: a wireless zone shows missing, supervision, or check trouble. 1. Is the sensor still there and in one piece? Customers sometimes remove sensors during painting or window replacement. 2. Check the sensor battery. Replace with the exact type the manufacturer lists. 3. Trip the sensor and watch signal strength in test mode. - Weak → something changed: new metal (appliance, mirror, foil insulation, metal blinds), a moved receiver, or a remodel. Relocate the sensor or receiver, or add a repeater. - No signal at all → confirm the serial number is enrolled to that zone. Re-enroll if needed. 4. Check the receiver: power, antennas, and location (not inside a metal can or next to the electrical panel). 5. If several sensors drop at once, suspect the receiver, RF jamming, or interference from new wireless equipment. 6. Clear the trouble per the panel's procedure and confirm the sensor checks in."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Failure to communicate with the central station","where":"Guide 8","href":"troubleshoot/8","text":"Symptom: panel shows communication failure, or the central station reports a missed periodic test or no signals. 1. Ask the central station what they last received and when. 2. Cellular: check signal strength at the communicator. Has something blocked it (a new metal door, panel moved)? Is the communicator activated and the account in good standing on the provider portal? Is it on a network that's been retired? → Remote antenna, relocate, or upgrade the communicator. 3. IP: is the customer's internet up? Did they change the router or internet provider? Check the Ethernet cable and link lights. The panel may need a new IP setting or DHCP. 4. Phone line (POTS): is there dial tone? Many landlines have been replaced by VoIP or fiber that may not pass alarm signals reliably. Recommend cellular. 5. Check programming: account number, receiver path, and reporting format match what the central station expects. 6. Send a test signal and confirm with the operator that it arrived with the right account."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Siren not sounding, or won't shut off","where":"Guide 9","href":"troubleshoot/9","text":"Symptom: no sound on alarm, or the siren keeps sounding after disarm. No sound 1. Check bell cutoff and siren programming (some installs or partitions are set to silent). 2. Meter the bell output during an alarm (on test with the central station). Expect about panel DC voltage. - Voltage present → wiring to the siren or the siren itself. Check polarity, splices, and the siren. - No voltage → check the bell fuse or PTC, the bell supervision EOL if the panel needs one, and the bell output programming. 3. Check total siren current against the bell output rating. Won't shut off 1. Disarm with a valid code. Still sounding? Check whether it's a different device (a smoke's built-in sounder, a separate system). 2. A 24-hour zone (fire, panic, tamper) may still be active. Clear the cause, then reset. 3. A tamper on an exterior siren box can keep it sounding; check the tamper and cover. 4. If you must silence it while troubleshooting, disconnect the siren wires at the panel and the self-contained siren's battery, and note it on the ticket."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Tamper troubles","where":"Guide 10","href":"troubleshoot/10","text":"Symptom: tamper trouble on a zone, keypad, module, siren, or panel. 1. Which device? Check the keypad or event history. 2. Panel or module cover: is the door closed and the tamper switch engaged? 3. Wireless sensor: is the cover snapped on fully? Was the sensor pulled off the wall? 4. DEOL zone tamper: the zone wiring reads open. Meter it (Guide 1). Check that both resistors are installed and correct. 5. Siren tamper: check the cover and tamper loop. 6. Clear the tamper per the panel; some need a code or a reset after the device restores."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Motion detector not triggering or over-triggering","where":"Guide 11","href":"troubleshoot/11","text":"Symptom: walk test doesn't trip the motion, or it trips with nothing there. Not triggering 1. Is the LED enabled for testing? (Many detectors disable it after setup.) 2. Meter power at the detector. Low or none → aux wiring or voltage drop. 3. Is it mounted at the rated height and aimed across the traffic path? Walking straight at a PIR is the weakest detection direction. 4. Is something blocking it (new shelving, a plant)? 5. Check jumper or DIP settings (pulse count, sensitivity, pet mode). 6. Meter the alarm contacts while walking in front of it. They should open. No change → replace the detector. Over-triggering → see Guide 3, step 3."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Ground fault","where":"Guide 12","href":"troubleshoot/12","text":"Symptom: panel shows a ground fault trouble. 1. Confirm the panel's earth ground connection is correct. 2. Disconnect zones and outputs one at a time (or in halves), clearing the trouble between each, until the ground fault clears. The last thing you removed has the fault. 3. Meter each conductor of that circuit to earth ground. Any reading means a wire is touching ground. 4. Look for a wire touching a metal box, a staple through a cable into a metal stud or conduit, water in an outdoor splice, or an outdoor device with damaged cable. 5. Repair, re-land, and confirm the trouble clears. These guides are a training aid. They don't replace the panel's installation manual, applicable codes, or your company's procedures."}]});
